WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:06.510 --> 00:00:09.180
- But now I get to introduce the next

00:00:09.180 --> 00:00:10.500
and this is really our last,

00:00:10.500 --> 00:00:12.460
it's not the last activity of the weekend,

00:00:12.460 --> 00:00:17.460
but it's the last major thought panel.

00:00:17.800 --> 00:00:20.420
And the focus instead of being on us

00:00:20.420 --> 00:00:22.780
and what co-education was like for us

00:00:22.780 --> 00:00:26.850
is much more institutional
what was co-education,

00:00:26.850 --> 00:00:30.390
what impact did it have,
what is it continuing to have

00:00:30.390 --> 00:00:34.180
and to the extent we have
a future looking panel,

00:00:34.180 --> 00:00:37.920
hopefully we will talk about
what does co-education mean

00:00:37.920 --> 00:00:39.963
today and even going forward.

00:00:41.020 --> 00:00:43.440
I have the opportunity
to introduce someone

00:00:43.440 --> 00:00:46.230
who has been introduced at
least four times so far,

00:00:46.230 --> 00:00:48.670
so I'm gonna keep my comments brief.

00:00:48.670 --> 00:00:52.420
Peter Salovey is the
23rd president of Yale.

00:00:52.420 --> 00:00:55.480
He has been president since 2013

00:00:55.480 --> 00:00:59.360
and I won't repeat anything
more than to welcome him up

00:00:59.360 --> 00:01:02.485
to introduce the panel moderator, thanks.

00:01:02.485 --> 00:01:05.652
(audience applauding)

00:01:09.900 --> 00:01:12.780
- Thank You Barbara,
good afternoon everyone.

00:01:12.780 --> 00:01:15.120
I'm proud to be president 23,

00:01:15.120 --> 00:01:17.390
I get enormous amounts of clothing

00:01:17.390 --> 00:01:19.090
particularly athletic wear

00:01:19.090 --> 00:01:22.220
with the number 23 on it as a result.

00:01:22.220 --> 00:01:26.890
And that's Michael Jordan and Kobe Bryant,

00:01:26.890 --> 00:01:29.880
so I tease Rick, I always
say well who was 22 anyway?

00:01:29.880 --> 00:01:33.150
I can't think of any 22s.

00:01:33.150 --> 00:01:36.080
It's a really an honor to be able to offer

00:01:36.080 --> 00:01:40.773
some introductory remarks
for Why Co-education Matters.

00:01:42.705 --> 00:01:46.460
At this point in the
50th anniversary weekend,

00:01:46.460 --> 00:01:50.640
you have been a part of
many crucial discussions

00:01:50.640 --> 00:01:53.847
about how co-education began at Yale,

00:01:53.847 --> 00:01:55.970
and what it was like for
the first trailblazers

00:01:55.970 --> 00:01:58.840
who transformed this university.

00:01:58.840 --> 00:02:02.730
And this afternoon our
panelists will explore further

00:02:02.730 --> 00:02:05.090
the meaning of this anniversary

00:02:05.090 --> 00:02:09.473
by examining past present
and future of co-education.

00:02:10.660 --> 00:02:12.070
I was thinking that it's hard

00:02:12.070 --> 00:02:16.090
to imagine a Yale without women.

00:02:16.090 --> 00:02:18.680
So the Yale College without women.

00:02:18.680 --> 00:02:23.680
Last May, 659 of the 1,350
undergraduate degrees

00:02:24.250 --> 00:02:27.860
awarded at commencement went to women.

00:02:27.860 --> 00:02:30.810
This Fall of course we
welcomed just as many women

00:02:30.810 --> 00:02:35.810
as men into the Yale
College class of 2023.

00:02:36.110 --> 00:02:39.350
By the way all of those first
years were wearing shirts

00:02:39.350 --> 00:02:43.480
that said 23 on it and I
thought, isn't this nice?

00:02:43.480 --> 00:02:45.370
They're greeting their president,

00:02:45.370 --> 00:02:47.320
of course it had nothing to do with me.

00:02:48.230 --> 00:02:52.520
Further today, today, right now, today,

00:02:52.520 --> 00:02:55.940
seven of the 15 Dean's of the
undergraduate professional

00:02:55.940 --> 00:02:59.330
and graduate schools are
women but in February,

00:02:59.330 --> 00:03:01.510
as I mentioned to some of you yesterday,

00:03:01.510 --> 00:03:04.590
that number goes to eight, more than half,

00:03:04.590 --> 00:03:06.450
with the addition of dr. Nancy Brown,

00:03:06.450 --> 00:03:10.023
the new Dean of the
Yale School of Medicine.

00:03:11.500 --> 00:03:14.420
The senior fellow of the Yale Corporation

00:03:14.420 --> 00:03:18.780
who is on this panel is a woman

00:03:18.780 --> 00:03:21.260
but so was the previous senior fellow

00:03:21.260 --> 00:03:25.290
and so was the senior fellow before her.

00:03:25.290 --> 00:03:27.861
Don't let the word fellow fool you.

00:03:27.861 --> 00:03:30.660
(audience applauding)

00:03:30.660 --> 00:03:34.240
Co-education has increased
the representation of women

00:03:34.240 --> 00:03:37.340
in every kind of role
at Yale in universities

00:03:37.340 --> 00:03:40.140
around the world, in government agencies,

00:03:40.140 --> 00:03:44.040
businesses and numerous
other sectors as well.

00:03:44.040 --> 00:03:46.090
The women who led co-education,

00:03:46.090 --> 00:03:50.340
who led the efforts in the 1960s and 70s

00:03:50.340 --> 00:03:54.020
set the stage for our efforts today

00:03:54.020 --> 00:03:56.220
to increase representation of women

00:03:56.220 --> 00:03:59.940
in faculty positions
and in leadership roles

00:03:59.940 --> 00:04:02.200
across all sectors.

00:04:02.200 --> 00:04:05.190
So these trailblazers are
the ones who taught us,

00:04:05.190 --> 00:04:07.900
you are the ones who taught us how to have

00:04:07.900 --> 00:04:11.110
deeper discussions about
inclusion, about diversity,

00:04:11.110 --> 00:04:14.200
about belonging for all individuals,

00:04:14.200 --> 00:04:16.870
for people of every gender and every way

00:04:16.870 --> 00:04:19.543
of understanding themselves.

00:04:20.740 --> 00:04:24.430
As we celebrate co-education,
we look to a future

00:04:24.430 --> 00:04:28.180
that's built on your work, your triumphs,

00:04:28.180 --> 00:04:31.840
your experiences, your
wisdom of what provide

00:04:31.840 --> 00:04:35.630
a foundation for students on campus today

00:04:35.630 --> 00:04:40.630
and they helped shape Yale and
the world in the years ahead.

00:04:41.690 --> 00:04:46.450
So I have to thank you
for the contributions

00:04:46.450 --> 00:04:51.330
that you have all made that
have improved higher education

00:04:51.330 --> 00:04:56.060
in scholarship and research
especially here at Yale

00:04:56.060 --> 00:04:59.240
but at institutions around this country

00:04:59.240 --> 00:05:01.580
indeed around the globe.

00:05:01.580 --> 00:05:03.210
And I wanna thank you, those of you

00:05:03.210 --> 00:05:07.200
who have remained interested in continuing

00:05:07.200 --> 00:05:12.200
to further higher education
especially the promise

00:05:12.500 --> 00:05:17.500
that the co-education of Yale
College presents to all of us.

00:05:18.760 --> 00:05:22.430
So it is a pleasure for me
to introduce the moderator

00:05:22.430 --> 00:05:25.050
of this afternoon's panel, Karen Lawrence,

00:05:25.050 --> 00:05:26.530
from the class of 1971.

00:05:27.663 --> 00:05:30.413
(all applauding)

00:05:33.640 --> 00:05:36.510
- Karen currently serves as the president

00:05:36.510 --> 00:05:38.560
of the Huntington Library Art Museum

00:05:38.560 --> 00:05:42.640
and Botanical Gardens in
San Marino California.

00:05:42.640 --> 00:05:46.130
Before that, she was the
president for a decade

00:05:46.130 --> 00:05:48.280
of Sarah Lawrence College.

00:05:48.280 --> 00:05:52.140
She's a renowned educator,
scholar, a leader.

00:05:52.140 --> 00:05:57.140
She typifies the qualities
that Yale aspires

00:05:57.990 --> 00:06:00.890
to inculcate among our students.

00:06:00.890 --> 00:06:03.320
So she will tell you about
the rest of the panel,

00:06:03.320 --> 00:06:07.130
but join me in welcoming
her and our colleagues

00:06:07.130 --> 00:06:09.156
here this afternoon, thank you.

00:06:09.156 --> 00:06:12.323
(audience applauding)

00:06:13.350 --> 00:06:17.110
- Thank You president Salovey
and it's a real pleasure...

00:06:17.110 --> 00:06:18.123
Can you hear me?

00:06:19.023 --> 00:06:23.713
Okay, I think we've got the
mics solved, so that's good.

00:06:24.590 --> 00:06:25.810
It's a pleasure to be here

00:06:25.810 --> 00:06:30.810
and to introduce our esteemed panelists.

00:06:31.010 --> 00:06:36.000
I do wanna say as a graduate of 1971

00:06:36.000 --> 00:06:39.180
that it's been a pleasure
to relieve the moments

00:06:39.180 --> 00:06:43.410
all together of our Fishbowl existence

00:06:43.410 --> 00:06:46.000
as it was called in the survey.

00:06:46.000 --> 00:06:50.780
And also, and the previous panel
was fabulous in doing that.

00:06:50.780 --> 00:06:54.480
And also to place it in
the cultural contexts

00:06:57.140 --> 00:06:58.820
in which its embedded.

00:06:58.820 --> 00:07:03.040
And Sam Chauncey's discussion last night,

00:07:03.040 --> 00:07:06.240
the fireside chat and
also the panel yesterday

00:07:06.240 --> 00:07:10.420
on Yale society in the 60s,
I think really provided

00:07:10.420 --> 00:07:12.580
that very important context.

00:07:12.580 --> 00:07:15.090
I wanna say one personal thing about

00:07:15.090 --> 00:07:18.873
that I think for most of us who were here,

00:07:20.020 --> 00:07:23.390
co-education is embedded in that context.

00:07:23.390 --> 00:07:27.060
And for me it's almost
impossible to think about it

00:07:27.060 --> 00:07:30.056
and my education without thinking

00:07:30.056 --> 00:07:35.056
about the political education and May Day.

00:07:35.460 --> 00:07:38.480
And so I just wanna share
one very quick vignette,

00:07:38.480 --> 00:07:42.280
I was in Davenport taking
a Davenport college seminar

00:07:42.280 --> 00:07:46.810
with Anna Freud, one
of the 15 lucky people

00:07:46.810 --> 00:07:51.050
students in Davenport who
could take the psychopathology

00:07:51.050 --> 00:07:55.460
of childhood with Anna
Freud and Silas Mann.

00:07:55.460 --> 00:07:59.290
And it was within ambivalence
that I faced that moment

00:07:59.290 --> 00:08:04.290
when everybody made the decision
and she was going to leave.

00:08:04.320 --> 00:08:08.880
But I was reminded at lunch
by one of my roommates

00:08:08.880 --> 00:08:11.670
that the day before May Day and I stayed

00:08:11.670 --> 00:08:16.140
and was involved in that with other folks.

00:08:16.140 --> 00:08:20.490
And the day before May
Day, Anna Freud came

00:08:20.490 --> 00:08:22.800
and spoke to the Davenport students

00:08:22.800 --> 00:08:26.140
about her experience in
the Blitz in World War II.

00:08:26.140 --> 00:08:29.620
And we gave her a Yale sweatshirt,

00:08:29.620 --> 00:08:33.810
and my most vivid memory
is of Anna Freud putting on

00:08:33.810 --> 00:08:38.330
a big sweatshirt with a Y over her dress

00:08:38.330 --> 00:08:43.330
and totally unselfconsciously
and smiling at everyone

00:08:43.790 --> 00:08:45.263
and then she left,

00:08:46.160 --> 00:08:49.390
and she left the Yale
campus understandably

00:08:49.390 --> 00:08:50.820
leaving at that moment.

00:08:50.820 --> 00:08:54.090
But for me, those memories
and for many of us

00:08:54.090 --> 00:08:57.690
I think are very embedded in that context.

00:08:57.690 --> 00:09:01.090
So today we are gonna
look back a little bit

00:09:01.090 --> 00:09:04.730
but really concentrate
as president Salovey said

00:09:04.730 --> 00:09:08.290
on the present and what's
the unfinished business

00:09:08.290 --> 00:09:11.510
of co-education both at Yale and elsewhere

00:09:11.510 --> 00:09:14.320
and what have we learned about it.

00:09:14.320 --> 00:09:18.750
You have the bios of
our impressive panelists

00:09:18.750 --> 00:09:20.143
in your programs.

00:09:21.140 --> 00:09:23.290
They are seasoned administrators,

00:09:23.290 --> 00:09:24.580
I don't know that I'm gonna ask you

00:09:24.580 --> 00:09:29.580
for the administrators
point of view but I'm sure--

00:09:29.682 --> 00:09:30.918
- Fascinating, though it would be.

00:09:30.918 --> 00:09:31.980
(audience laughing)

00:09:31.980 --> 00:09:33.830
- Fascinating that would be, he says.

00:09:35.115 --> 00:09:38.350
But everything that you've been and done

00:09:38.350 --> 00:09:42.840
will be part of what you
know and your response.

00:09:42.840 --> 00:09:46.540
I'm gonna just focus on their
multiple connections to Yale

00:09:46.540 --> 00:09:51.540
and experiences of almost
different Yale's that they've had.

00:09:51.890 --> 00:09:55.440
So Cathy or Catharine Bond Hill

00:09:55.440 --> 00:09:57.910
is the former president of Vassar

00:09:57.910 --> 00:10:02.910
and currently managing
director of Ithaka S+R Research

00:10:04.280 --> 00:10:07.330
and Consulting Initiatives
to broaden access

00:10:07.330 --> 00:10:09.070
to higher education.

00:10:09.070 --> 00:10:14.070
She received her Ph.D. in
economics from Yale in 1985

00:10:14.320 --> 00:10:17.960
at a time when I'm betting
you were one of the few women

00:10:17.960 --> 00:10:22.130
at least, one of two
women in your classes.

00:10:22.130 --> 00:10:26.220
She has the vantage point of now serving

00:10:26.220 --> 00:10:29.490
on the board of Yale and U.S.

00:10:29.490 --> 00:10:34.490
and is successor and senior
trustee of the Yale Corporation

00:10:34.520 --> 00:10:39.520
and is professor emeritus of,
president emeritus of Vassar.

00:10:41.440 --> 00:10:45.660
Kim Golf Crews experienced
Yale as an undergraduate

00:10:45.660 --> 00:10:48.840
about 10 years after we did.

00:10:48.840 --> 00:10:53.400
She graduated in '83 and
went to Yale Law School

00:10:53.400 --> 00:10:57.200
and graduated from the Law School in '86.

00:10:57.200 --> 00:11:00.950
She previously served as
assistant dean in the college

00:11:00.950 --> 00:11:04.590
and director of the
Afro-American Cultural Center.

00:11:04.590 --> 00:11:07.960
And now her vantage point
is as vice president

00:11:07.960 --> 00:11:11.680
for student life at Yale and secretary

00:11:11.680 --> 00:11:13.143
of the Yale Corporation.

00:11:14.270 --> 00:11:17.820
Marvin Chun serves as Dean of Yale College

00:11:17.820 --> 00:11:21.340
and is the Richard M. Colgate
Professor of Psychology

00:11:21.340 --> 00:11:23.500
with appointments in cognitive science

00:11:23.500 --> 00:11:27.450
in the Yale School of Medicine
Department of neuroscience.

00:11:27.450 --> 00:11:30.853
He's also served as head
of Berkeley College.

00:11:31.820 --> 00:11:36.000
I think Dick Brodhead
president emeritus of Duke

00:11:36.000 --> 00:11:39.920
wins the award for the most
varied relation to Yale

00:11:39.920 --> 00:11:42.260
on this panel at least.

00:11:42.260 --> 00:11:47.260
He graduated from an all-male Yale in 1968

00:11:47.900 --> 00:11:51.780
and was a graduate student
and a teaching assistant

00:11:51.780 --> 00:11:55.140
and then a faculty member
all while we were going

00:11:55.140 --> 00:11:59.450
through our education and served long

00:11:59.450 --> 00:12:03.270
and very successfully
as Dean of Yale College

00:12:03.270 --> 00:12:06.960
and has the most comprehensive
experience, I think,

00:12:06.960 --> 00:12:08.020
of different Yale's.

00:12:08.020 --> 00:12:10.320
So let me, I do wanna look back

00:12:10.320 --> 00:12:14.710
and I do wanna talk about
briefly, that transition.

00:12:14.710 --> 00:12:17.760
And bringing to bear
your other experiences

00:12:17.760 --> 00:12:19.980
but certainly the transitional experiences

00:12:19.980 --> 00:12:21.830
for you at that moment.

00:12:21.830 --> 00:12:24.703
- Well, I've been thinking
about this and let me say,

00:12:26.292 --> 00:12:28.740
you know listening to me
is to me always fascinating

00:12:28.740 --> 00:12:30.540
but being here this
morning and hearing people

00:12:30.540 --> 00:12:33.200
tell all those particular personal stories

00:12:33.200 --> 00:12:35.330
of where different people
found their friends,

00:12:35.330 --> 00:12:37.270
where different people
found their opportunities

00:12:37.270 --> 00:12:39.410
in their futures that was just so fun.

00:12:39.410 --> 00:12:41.420
So I'll just whirlwind my two cents.

00:12:41.420 --> 00:12:43.540
I came to Yale in 1964,

00:12:43.540 --> 00:12:44.870
well maybe I'll start this way.

00:12:44.870 --> 00:12:48.140
I was president of Duke for 13
years and while I was there,

00:12:48.140 --> 00:12:50.270
we spent a year and a half celebrating

00:12:50.270 --> 00:12:53.310
a deeply significant 50th anniversary,

00:12:53.310 --> 00:12:55.370
the 50th anniversary of the admission

00:12:55.370 --> 00:12:58.050
of the first African
American students to do.

00:12:58.050 --> 00:13:00.530
Now you can say, how is that possible?

00:13:00.530 --> 00:13:02.890
But it was a segregated
state let's remember

00:13:02.890 --> 00:13:05.060
and although I went to
Yale at the same time,

00:13:05.060 --> 00:13:08.230
Yale was not segregated but
there were 10 black students

00:13:08.230 --> 00:13:09.850
in a class of 1,000 when I was there.

00:13:09.850 --> 00:13:12.500
So it was something, it was something.

00:13:12.500 --> 00:13:16.200
Now the interesting thing
is when say when Duke

00:13:16.200 --> 00:13:19.920
was integrated for
college students in 1963,

00:13:19.920 --> 00:13:21.790
this was after there had already been 10

00:13:21.790 --> 00:13:25.580
or maybe even 15 years
of very active agitation

00:13:25.580 --> 00:13:28.240
about the issues of racial exclusion

00:13:28.240 --> 00:13:29.620
not only on the Duke campus

00:13:30.617 --> 00:13:31.570
but across the South in Durham,

00:13:31.570 --> 00:13:33.390
itself had been a center of this agitation

00:13:33.390 --> 00:13:35.730
going back to the early 1950s.

00:13:35.730 --> 00:13:37.970
Now I put that in my
mind and I compared it

00:13:37.970 --> 00:13:40.040
to what it was like to raise the subject

00:13:40.040 --> 00:13:44.470
of the admission of women
to Yale in 1965, '6 '7.

00:13:44.470 --> 00:13:46.890
And here's why I got
to tell you, you know,

00:13:46.890 --> 00:13:49.870
I was a smart person, my
classmates were smart people

00:13:49.870 --> 00:13:51.750
but you know I just will admit it to you,

00:13:51.750 --> 00:13:53.760
when I came here I did not come here

00:13:53.760 --> 00:13:56.980
because it was an all-male
school or in hopes

00:13:56.980 --> 00:13:58.720
that it would soon be co-educated.

00:13:58.720 --> 00:14:01.620
You know, my classmates
understood that Yale was all-male,

00:14:01.620 --> 00:14:05.310
it had already been for
200 and what, do the math,

00:14:05.310 --> 00:14:09.250
263 years by that point
so was every other school

00:14:09.250 --> 00:14:11.120
we would have gone to
if we hadn't gone here.

00:14:11.120 --> 00:14:13.690
And so if you asked a
male student at that time,

00:14:13.690 --> 00:14:15.760
why does Yale not have women?

00:14:15.760 --> 00:14:17.890
The answer would have been I think just,

00:14:17.890 --> 00:14:19.140
I'll just put it more positively,

00:14:19.140 --> 00:14:20.870
why does Yale admit only man?

00:14:20.870 --> 00:14:23.210
I think the answer would
have been, because it does,

00:14:23.210 --> 00:14:25.300
because that's just the way it is.

00:14:25.300 --> 00:14:27.990
None of us like thought
much more deeply about that.

00:14:27.990 --> 00:14:30.450
If you had said to any
member of my class that Yale

00:14:30.450 --> 00:14:32.810
admitted only man because it had a mission

00:14:32.810 --> 00:14:35.720
to produce 1,000 male leaders,
we all regarded that phrase

00:14:35.720 --> 00:14:39.250
with great scorn and dripping sarcasm

00:14:39.250 --> 00:14:40.850
whenever we mentioned it.

00:14:40.850 --> 00:14:43.820
So here was a place
that was all male, why?

00:14:43.820 --> 00:14:45.800
Because it just was.

00:14:45.800 --> 00:14:47.180
That was true in my freshman year,

00:14:47.180 --> 00:14:49.270
I do not remember a
discussion of the subject

00:14:49.270 --> 00:14:51.350
in my freshman year, I
don't remember a discussion

00:14:51.350 --> 00:14:54.250
in my sophomore year, I
can remember the beginning

00:14:54.250 --> 00:14:56.220
of discussions in my junior year.

00:14:56.220 --> 00:14:58.210
And then it falls in
the category of things

00:14:58.210 --> 00:15:00.780
that went from being
unthinkable to mandatory

00:15:00.780 --> 00:15:03.390
without ever passing through
the phase of reflection.

00:15:03.390 --> 00:15:07.110
All of a sudden me and
everybody I knew understood

00:15:07.110 --> 00:15:09.120
that it was now totally
wrong for this place

00:15:09.120 --> 00:15:12.350
to be exclusive of women and
then it had to admit women

00:15:12.350 --> 00:15:13.788
as quickly as possible.

00:15:13.788 --> 00:15:16.960
It was in my senior year the
co-education week took place,

00:15:16.960 --> 00:15:19.640
obviously for later taught my
wife Constitutional Law is,

00:15:19.640 --> 00:15:24.260
you know, I knew him, we
all participated in it.

00:15:24.260 --> 00:15:26.790
And people in my village
believe that the reason

00:15:26.790 --> 00:15:30.170
Yale co-education is because
it was about to be co educated

00:15:30.170 --> 00:15:33.010
by its students if it did not make

00:15:33.010 --> 00:15:36.090
the administrative concession do that.

00:15:36.090 --> 00:15:38.900
So there it is, it went from unthinkable

00:15:38.900 --> 00:15:40.710
to has to happen and sure enough

00:15:40.710 --> 00:15:42.810
it happened very quickly thereafter.

00:15:42.810 --> 00:15:45.290
But so now I just, you
know, by now you've heard

00:15:45.290 --> 00:15:47.250
the reasons why Yale co-educated.

00:15:47.250 --> 00:15:49.750
Nancy Malkiel gave a
very thought-provoking

00:15:49.750 --> 00:15:51.600
and I believe historically accurate

00:15:51.600 --> 00:15:55.140
strategic account of the
administration's logic.

00:15:55.140 --> 00:15:58.100
And I could give and you
could give an account

00:15:58.100 --> 00:16:00.420
based on morality and social justice

00:16:00.420 --> 00:16:03.810
or even the social policy of
why Yale needed to co-educate.

00:16:03.810 --> 00:16:05.590
But I'm just gonna give you the reasons

00:16:05.590 --> 00:16:08.520
why it was important to a young man

00:16:08.520 --> 00:16:10.100
who was a student at that time.

00:16:10.100 --> 00:16:13.730
And there'd be twofold,
one if you went here,

00:16:13.730 --> 00:16:15.600
you had a lot of great male friends

00:16:15.600 --> 00:16:19.060
but friendship with women
was an unknown phenomenon.

00:16:19.060 --> 00:16:21.810
Really pretty much that's
true, if you think about it.

00:16:21.810 --> 00:16:24.280
I had also gone to an
all-male school for four years

00:16:24.280 --> 00:16:27.890
before I came here, you're
used to schools being all-male

00:16:27.890 --> 00:16:29.990
and then occasionally
something happened in college

00:16:29.990 --> 00:16:31.910
that never happened in boarding school

00:16:31.910 --> 00:16:35.410
which is buses would arrive
and women would arrive

00:16:35.410 --> 00:16:37.080
and they would go up the
stairs and then you go

00:16:37.080 --> 00:16:38.640
and ask them to dance and whatever you can

00:16:38.640 --> 00:16:40.730
possibly accomplish beyond that point.

00:16:40.730 --> 00:16:42.700
And then it was all over.

00:16:42.700 --> 00:16:44.730
And as I say, I mean I'd have to say,

00:16:44.730 --> 00:16:46.760
no one regarded this
I was fun at the time,

00:16:46.760 --> 00:16:48.800
it was very distasteful.

00:16:48.800 --> 00:16:51.090
There was something, you know,
I don't wanna use the word

00:16:51.090 --> 00:16:52.310
but it's not too strong of a word,

00:16:52.310 --> 00:16:53.930
there was something debasing.

00:16:53.930 --> 00:16:57.330
Not only for the victims of
it but for oneself as well.

00:16:57.330 --> 00:16:58.870
And I did not regard road trips,

00:16:58.870 --> 00:17:00.467
I mean you know Vassar meant road trips,

00:17:00.467 --> 00:17:02.330
and I did not regard road trips

00:17:02.330 --> 00:17:04.460
as that much more fun either.

00:17:04.460 --> 00:17:06.972
It was just the nature, you know,

00:17:06.972 --> 00:17:08.270
did you ever read The Scarlet Letter,

00:17:08.270 --> 00:17:10.210
remember what Hester Prynne
wants to do at the end?

00:17:10.210 --> 00:17:14.090
She wants a utopian colony
where someone will understand

00:17:14.090 --> 00:17:16.560
how to put the relations of men and women

00:17:16.560 --> 00:17:19.570
on a surer ground of mutual happiness.

00:17:19.570 --> 00:17:21.210
That's what Yale needed at that point.

00:17:21.210 --> 00:17:23.350
The relations of men and
women were on a ground

00:17:23.350 --> 00:17:25.953
of total abnormality, total unnaturalness.

00:17:26.958 --> 00:17:29.000
And so it seems to me, I'd have to say

00:17:29.000 --> 00:17:30.970
in my own personal life, reaching a state

00:17:30.970 --> 00:17:33.010
where women could be just your friends,

00:17:33.010 --> 00:17:34.690
where they could know you just as you

00:17:34.690 --> 00:17:36.940
and you could know them just as them,

00:17:36.940 --> 00:17:39.290
that was actually something
it took co-education

00:17:39.290 --> 00:17:41.260
to produce at this University.

00:17:41.260 --> 00:17:43.500
By chance, I believe I attended

00:17:43.500 --> 00:17:47.370
the first fully co-educated
class in year college.

00:17:47.370 --> 00:17:49.640
When I was a senior, I had to take Latin

00:17:49.640 --> 00:17:51.440
because I was going to
go to graduate school

00:17:51.440 --> 00:17:55.150
and you had to have passed
tests in many languages.

00:17:55.150 --> 00:17:57.540
So I took first year Latin as a senior

00:17:57.540 --> 00:17:59.640
but the class was filled with women.

00:17:59.640 --> 00:18:01.810
In the graduate school, they're to pass

00:18:01.810 --> 00:18:04.110
their language requirements
who were graduate students

00:18:04.110 --> 00:18:06.330
in music and French,
Nancy Vicker's friends

00:18:06.330 --> 00:18:08.110
were in this class.

00:18:08.110 --> 00:18:10.110
And I thought all of a
sudden you're at a class

00:18:10.110 --> 00:18:13.150
where half or more of the
students were women and you know,

00:18:13.150 --> 00:18:15.260
we just became friends
and that was the beginning

00:18:15.260 --> 00:18:19.270
of a whole new chapter of
maturity in my education.

00:18:19.270 --> 00:18:21.530
So it seems to me in some sense,

00:18:21.530 --> 00:18:26.200
it was about humanizing
life for co-education.

00:18:26.200 --> 00:18:27.870
But then I discovered a second reason

00:18:27.870 --> 00:18:32.720
that I still wouldn't have
been aware of in 1968.

00:18:32.720 --> 00:18:35.470
You've heard the strange fact,
I entered graduate school

00:18:35.470 --> 00:18:39.040
in the Fall of '68, my
classmates were at least

00:18:39.040 --> 00:18:41.540
half women there and I
immediately had many women friends

00:18:41.540 --> 00:18:42.940
including one who I married.

00:18:44.491 --> 00:18:48.600
That Fall, it was announced
that Yale would admit women,

00:18:48.600 --> 00:18:51.240
the following Fall women were admitted

00:18:51.240 --> 00:18:52.510
and the following Fall,

00:18:52.510 --> 00:18:54.960
I drew my first to teaching assignment.

00:18:54.960 --> 00:18:56.730
Now please keep in mind, I am a person

00:18:56.730 --> 00:18:59.110
who had only ever wanted
to do one thing on earth,

00:18:59.110 --> 00:19:00.430
and that's probably still true,

00:19:00.430 --> 00:19:02.640
which was to be a college teacher

00:19:02.640 --> 00:19:04.810
but I never done it, you know.

00:19:04.810 --> 00:19:08.180
And so all of a sudden, in
Alvin Kern and Shakespeare class

00:19:08.180 --> 00:19:11.240
I had to lead a discussion
section of my first class.

00:19:11.240 --> 00:19:13.270
But by that point the class already had

00:19:13.270 --> 00:19:14.990
a significant number of women,

00:19:14.990 --> 00:19:16.620
Lydia Wegman is here somewhere.

00:19:16.620 --> 00:19:17.453
Where are you?

00:19:17.453 --> 00:19:18.660
There you are, you were in my class.

00:19:18.660 --> 00:19:20.860
Is there anybody else
who was in that class?

00:19:22.620 --> 00:19:24.420
Where are the rest of you?

00:19:24.420 --> 00:19:26.683
Yeah, you, who are you?

00:19:27.890 --> 00:19:29.510
Yell me your name.

00:19:29.510 --> 00:19:34.093
(background noise drowns other sounds)

00:19:41.570 --> 00:19:42.720
I might have been, all right.

00:19:42.720 --> 00:19:44.530
So this room contains my actual

00:19:44.530 --> 00:19:46.870
and my potential students, okay.

00:19:46.870 --> 00:19:48.900
But anyway, but here's the funny thing,

00:19:48.900 --> 00:19:50.720
so when I started teaching of course,

00:19:50.720 --> 00:19:52.450
you know this was a discussion section,

00:19:52.450 --> 00:19:54.650
it was not a lecture, so
you went in and tried to ask

00:19:54.650 --> 00:19:56.380
interesting and provocative questions

00:19:56.380 --> 00:19:58.810
and right away you found
this is a piece of cake

00:19:58.810 --> 00:20:00.320
because people would
just raise their hands

00:20:00.320 --> 00:20:01.860
and plunge in and begin to discuss

00:20:01.860 --> 00:20:03.550
all these interesting things.

00:20:03.550 --> 00:20:06.530
But I'd have to say, the
discussions in that class

00:20:06.530 --> 00:20:08.240
were different than they had been

00:20:08.240 --> 00:20:09.950
when I had been an undergraduate

00:20:09.950 --> 00:20:13.140
two short years before in all-male Yale.

00:20:13.140 --> 00:20:15.620
I never was a student in co-educated Yale

00:20:15.620 --> 00:20:18.820
and I never was a teacher
in an non co-educated Yale.

00:20:18.820 --> 00:20:20.800
But right away in that
class you saw, you know,

00:20:20.800 --> 00:20:23.980
women light class discussion,
they raised their hands,

00:20:23.980 --> 00:20:26.070
they plunged right into it.

00:20:26.070 --> 00:20:28.470
The students in my class
nothing reminded me of them

00:20:28.470 --> 00:20:31.180
so much as hearing the people
on the panel this morning.

00:20:31.180 --> 00:20:33.500
All that vitality, all
that articulateness,

00:20:33.500 --> 00:20:35.720
all that confident, the
confidence, I just desire

00:20:35.720 --> 00:20:36.990
to go in and get at it.

00:20:36.990 --> 00:20:38.910
And you know it had the funny thing

00:20:38.910 --> 00:20:41.470
which is it actually made
men behave differently

00:20:41.470 --> 00:20:45.770
in class discussion because
male Yale up 'til 1968

00:20:45.770 --> 00:20:49.160
was under the tyranny of
very oppressive stereotypes

00:20:49.160 --> 00:20:50.670
for men within the college,

00:20:50.670 --> 00:20:53.730
one of which is, it's
good to be intelligent

00:20:53.730 --> 00:20:56.410
but it's not good to
care too much about it.

00:20:56.410 --> 00:20:58.980
It's good to be intelligent but
not good to be intellectual.

00:20:58.980 --> 00:21:02.190
And so there was a kind of
pose of what shall we call it,

00:21:02.190 --> 00:21:05.770
relaxation, gentlemanly leisure.

00:21:05.770 --> 00:21:08.440
I could if I would, you
know what I'm talking about

00:21:08.440 --> 00:21:11.280
and actually the interesting
thing is once the class started

00:21:11.280 --> 00:21:13.930
and men were occupying the
pose of gentlemanly leisure

00:21:13.930 --> 00:21:15.820
and women were making all
the interesting points,

00:21:15.820 --> 00:21:17.360
that wasn't gonna last very long.

00:21:17.360 --> 00:21:20.550
And soon the men discovered
that they not only wanted

00:21:20.550 --> 00:21:22.750
to do this too but could do it too.

00:21:22.750 --> 00:21:24.270
And I'd have to say,
I'm not sure this point

00:21:24.270 --> 00:21:26.490
has been made as deeply
as I would make it,

00:21:26.490 --> 00:21:29.960
the deepest change that
co-education made for Yale

00:21:29.960 --> 00:21:32.480
is that it made it an academically vibrant

00:21:32.480 --> 00:21:34.734
and vital place for its undergraduate.

00:21:34.734 --> 00:21:37.901
(audience applauding)

00:21:41.133 --> 00:21:42.490
I would never, let me say what,

00:21:42.490 --> 00:21:43.630
don't show me the yellow card yet,

00:21:43.630 --> 00:21:45.980
I've got one more thing
to say, no yellow card.

00:21:46.920 --> 00:21:47.770
Just one more thing.

00:21:47.770 --> 00:21:49.160
And in those classes the whole point

00:21:49.160 --> 00:21:51.380
is all of a sudden you
had women in the classes,

00:21:51.380 --> 00:21:53.120
the point has been made many times,

00:21:53.120 --> 00:21:56.750
no one was ever there to
be called on or to speak

00:21:56.750 --> 00:21:58.220
the woman's point of view.

00:21:58.220 --> 00:21:59.170
It would never have occurred to me

00:21:59.170 --> 00:22:00.840
to ask a student to say that.

00:22:00.840 --> 00:22:03.530
Nevertheless, if you have
excluded half of humanity

00:22:03.530 --> 00:22:05.080
from your class for this point,

00:22:05.080 --> 00:22:06.920
you may be sure there's
gonna be points of view

00:22:06.920 --> 00:22:08.730
now present that used to be absent.

00:22:08.730 --> 00:22:10.840
And so it was not only the vibrancy

00:22:10.840 --> 00:22:13.450
but also the fullness of consideration

00:22:13.450 --> 00:22:14.480
that was changed there.

00:22:14.480 --> 00:22:15.820
And then I remember
something that happened

00:22:15.820 --> 00:22:17.920
in my second year and I'm
trying to make eye contact

00:22:17.920 --> 00:22:19.470
with Margaret Hohman (mumbles),

00:22:20.760 --> 00:22:23.740
you know feminism came
together with co-education

00:22:23.740 --> 00:22:26.231
into the life of male students at Yale.

00:22:26.231 --> 00:22:29.510
And an interesting thing is
I couldn't remember teaching,

00:22:29.510 --> 00:22:31.930
did any of you ever take English 25,

00:22:31.930 --> 00:22:35.710
Chaucer, Spenser, Milton, Pope.

00:22:35.710 --> 00:22:38.250
Remember there's a poem by
Pope that we all used to teach

00:22:38.250 --> 00:22:41.680
called Epistle To A Lady
on the character of women.

00:22:41.680 --> 00:22:43.017
Remember its first line,

00:22:43.017 --> 00:22:46.840
"Nothing so true as
what you once let fall,

00:22:46.840 --> 00:22:50.332
most women have no character at all."

00:22:50.332 --> 00:22:51.165
(audience laughing)

00:22:51.165 --> 00:22:53.810
And then it goes on and it's
a very witty brilliant poem.

00:22:53.810 --> 00:22:55.490
And at the end of the
class, I remember a student,

00:22:55.490 --> 00:22:57.530
I'll tell you her name
though I do not know her

00:22:57.530 --> 00:23:00.080
since she graduated, Judith
Fabricant was her name,

00:23:00.080 --> 00:23:02.187
very smart, she raised her hand and said,

00:23:02.187 --> 00:23:04.450
"Nevertheless, what do
you think of somebody

00:23:04.450 --> 00:23:08.360
who would say nothing so true
was what you once left fall,

00:23:08.360 --> 00:23:10.860
most women have no character at all?"

00:23:10.860 --> 00:23:12.830
And you know, just all of
a sudden I'd have to say,

00:23:12.830 --> 00:23:16.550
at that moment you saw the
scales fell from your eyes

00:23:16.550 --> 00:23:19.350
and you realized the implicit selectivity,

00:23:19.350 --> 00:23:21.410
the implicit limitations of the things

00:23:21.410 --> 00:23:23.170
that had been taken for granted as true

00:23:23.170 --> 00:23:24.580
by everybody up to that point.

00:23:24.580 --> 00:23:26.930
That was the biggest maybe later changes

00:23:26.930 --> 00:23:28.305
but this is the very end, the end.

00:23:28.305 --> 00:23:30.070
- We'll begin tomorrow of the curriculum

00:23:30.070 --> 00:23:31.560
and how it changed with women.

00:23:31.560 --> 00:23:33.120
But I do wanna...

00:23:33.120 --> 00:23:34.193
Yeah.

00:23:34.193 --> 00:23:37.360
(audience applauding)

00:23:39.470 --> 00:23:41.200
In a couple of the panels,

00:23:41.200 --> 00:23:45.060
we were talking about when Yale changed.

00:23:45.060 --> 00:23:48.610
And I do wanna ask actually whether

00:23:48.610 --> 00:23:51.110
one of the things that emerged
of the cultural context

00:23:51.110 --> 00:23:54.410
was that there were more public school men

00:23:54.410 --> 00:23:58.410
who came to Yale leading
up to co-education

00:23:58.410 --> 00:24:02.260
who might not have found it as bizarre

00:24:02.260 --> 00:24:04.597
to have women in the classroom.

00:24:04.597 --> 00:24:09.597
And so I just kind of
wanna ask about that point

00:24:10.880 --> 00:24:13.740
that there were antecedent conditions--

00:24:13.740 --> 00:24:15.090
- Oh clearly, they were.

00:24:15.090 --> 00:24:16.850
Clearly, they were.
- Or faculty who began

00:24:16.850 --> 00:24:20.520
to be hired from Midwest schools where--

00:24:20.520 --> 00:24:22.610
- Sure, and you can go read the history,

00:24:22.610 --> 00:24:25.580
the thing is in the class
of '67 was the first class

00:24:25.580 --> 00:24:28.440
that had a majority of
public school matriculants.

00:24:28.440 --> 00:24:31.700
There had been as many as
65% from private schools,

00:24:31.700 --> 00:24:32.980
from private boarding schools.

00:24:32.980 --> 00:24:34.640
And I think at one point I heard,

00:24:34.640 --> 00:24:36.640
more than 50% of Yale students

00:24:36.640 --> 00:24:39.590
used to come from the same eight schools.

00:24:39.590 --> 00:24:41.830
I went to end over, there were 45 people

00:24:41.830 --> 00:24:44.240
from my high school class
in my freshman class

00:24:44.240 --> 00:24:45.110
when I came here.

00:24:45.110 --> 00:24:48.370
Guess what, the next year
there were 22 from that school

00:24:48.370 --> 00:24:50.790
and it never reached
such a high number again.

00:24:50.790 --> 00:24:52.710
And surely that change helped a bit.

00:24:52.710 --> 00:24:54.321
It's all part of a larger change.

00:24:54.321 --> 00:24:55.154
- Right.

00:24:55.154 --> 00:24:56.453
- I'm just describing my
personal experience of it.

00:24:56.453 --> 00:24:58.117
- All right, thank you.

00:24:59.114 --> 00:25:01.930
When we talk about co-education,

00:25:01.930 --> 00:25:04.210
I think Sam Chauncey was quoted

00:25:04.210 --> 00:25:08.267
in the recent alumni magazine as saying,

00:25:08.267 --> 00:25:10.310
"People keep talking about co-education

00:25:10.310 --> 00:25:11.860
as if this was some magical thing

00:25:11.860 --> 00:25:15.620
that never happened before anywhere else."

00:25:15.620 --> 00:25:20.260
And we're here because
it was crucial to us

00:25:20.260 --> 00:25:23.610
to the institution that is
what we're talking about.

00:25:23.610 --> 00:25:28.320
It is true that in 1955,
75% of all colleges

00:25:28.320 --> 00:25:30.500
and universities enrolled both

00:25:30.500 --> 00:25:33.750
male and female undergraduates.

00:25:33.750 --> 00:25:36.720
And that often comparisons are made

00:25:36.720 --> 00:25:39.730
and there's wonderful
literature on comparisons

00:25:39.730 --> 00:25:42.763
between elite men's schools.

00:25:43.680 --> 00:25:46.070
And sometimes in the
literature comparisons

00:25:46.070 --> 00:25:50.040
about the benefits of
co-education for women

00:25:50.040 --> 00:25:52.860
versus single sex education.

00:25:52.860 --> 00:25:55.880
So these are parts of the discussion.

00:25:55.880 --> 00:25:58.930
I wanna turn to Cathy
who was the president

00:25:58.930 --> 00:26:03.760
of a college that went
co-ed the other way around

00:26:03.760 --> 00:26:08.760
and admitted men as a minority
to a majority female campus.

00:26:10.400 --> 00:26:13.360
And I share that with
you as Sarah Lawrence,

00:26:13.360 --> 00:26:17.720
Vassar received Yale's
proposal as did Sarah Lawrence,

00:26:17.720 --> 00:26:21.520
I mean rejected Gail's proposal

00:26:21.520 --> 00:26:24.470
as Sarah Lawrence rejected Princeton's.

00:26:24.470 --> 00:26:27.710
Can you talk a little bit
about that process and outcome.

00:26:27.710 --> 00:26:29.427
- Sure, so it's been wonderful to be here

00:26:29.427 --> 00:26:31.760
and it was so interesting
to hear people this morning.

00:26:31.760 --> 00:26:33.100
My own personal experience

00:26:33.100 --> 00:26:35.670
is actually more similar to yours.

00:26:35.670 --> 00:26:39.630
I was in the second class of
all women at Williams College

00:26:40.470 --> 00:26:43.920
which moved to co-education
a little bit more slowly

00:26:43.920 --> 00:26:46.690
than Yale but not by much
of some my entering class

00:26:46.690 --> 00:26:49.210
was the fall of '72
and I graduated in '76.

00:26:49.210 --> 00:26:52.263
So very, a lot of very common experiences.

00:26:53.210 --> 00:26:56.080
And then I went back to Williams
to teach and was Provost

00:26:56.080 --> 00:26:57.950
and then went to the Vassar position.

00:26:57.950 --> 00:26:59.340
When I went from Williams to Vassar,

00:26:59.340 --> 00:27:01.810
of course I dug into
the archives to find out

00:27:01.810 --> 00:27:05.120
about this period of time
when both Williams went co-ed

00:27:05.120 --> 00:27:08.253
and Yale went co-ed and Vassar went co-ed.

00:27:08.253 --> 00:27:11.070
And it was really fun to learn

00:27:11.070 --> 00:27:12.823
a little bit about the history.

00:27:14.250 --> 00:27:16.700
It had really started in the early 60s

00:27:16.700 --> 00:27:18.530
and in the archive so you
could see the presidents

00:27:18.530 --> 00:27:20.670
starting to really worry
about what was gonna happen

00:27:20.670 --> 00:27:23.730
as it was clear the
demographics were changing

00:27:23.730 --> 00:27:27.180
and what students were interested
in was changing as well.

00:27:27.180 --> 00:27:28.680
I couldn't get to town in time

00:27:28.680 --> 00:27:30.030
to hear Nancy's talk yesterday

00:27:30.030 --> 00:27:32.050
but we actually talked quite a lot

00:27:32.050 --> 00:27:33.280
when she was working on her book

00:27:33.280 --> 00:27:36.480
because she was trying to
dig into the Vassar data

00:27:36.480 --> 00:27:39.380
on what happened to the student body

00:27:39.380 --> 00:27:41.190
during this period of time.

00:27:41.190 --> 00:27:43.760
So anyway I learned a lot
about Vassar going co-ed

00:27:43.760 --> 00:27:47.883
and just some interesting
stories, I think.

00:27:48.760 --> 00:27:50.330
Very early on when I was at Vassar,

00:27:50.330 --> 00:27:52.720
I was meeting with the class of '69

00:27:52.720 --> 00:27:55.920
and they were the last
all-female class at Vassar

00:27:55.920 --> 00:27:58.960
about which they were quite proud.

00:27:58.960 --> 00:28:01.070
But it was so interesting the
first time I met with them

00:28:01.070 --> 00:28:02.530
because they were very divided

00:28:02.530 --> 00:28:06.020
on what right path was for Vassar.

00:28:06.020 --> 00:28:08.590
Some of them were just completely appalled

00:28:08.590 --> 00:28:12.840
by the notion that that Vassar
would have merged with Yale,

00:28:12.840 --> 00:28:16.490
some of them were quite appalled
that it was gonna go co-ed

00:28:16.490 --> 00:28:19.110
and some of them just were really sad

00:28:19.110 --> 00:28:21.680
that they didn't get a
chance to have a Yale degree.

00:28:21.680 --> 00:28:26.680
So it was this very mixed
history of that class.

00:28:27.460 --> 00:28:31.300
So Vassar goes co-ed, it goes co-ed

00:28:31.300 --> 00:28:33.140
for many of the same
reasons that Williams did

00:28:33.140 --> 00:28:35.350
which was that it was clear
and I think Nancy's book

00:28:35.350 --> 00:28:37.710
makes this point that
the world had changed

00:28:37.710 --> 00:28:39.640
and it was going to be
very hard to survive

00:28:39.640 --> 00:28:43.940
in an isolated community as
a single sex institution.

00:28:43.940 --> 00:28:48.170
What it did at Vassar was
changed the pool of students

00:28:48.170 --> 00:28:50.580
who were applying to go there.

00:28:50.580 --> 00:28:54.100
Before for many of you, I
think many of you actually

00:28:54.100 --> 00:28:57.380
started at one of the
women's, the Seven Sisters

00:28:57.380 --> 00:28:59.970
and then transferred, you
didn't have the option

00:28:59.970 --> 00:29:02.800
of going to Princeton or
Yale or Williams or Amherst.

00:29:02.800 --> 00:29:05.640
Once those opportunities arose you made

00:29:05.640 --> 00:29:08.960
a more decided decision about
what kind of an institution

00:29:08.960 --> 00:29:10.590
you wanted to go to.

00:29:10.590 --> 00:29:12.720
And I would say that
what happened at Vassar

00:29:12.720 --> 00:29:16.180
was that the applicant
pool changed from women

00:29:16.180 --> 00:29:19.300
who would have applied
to Princeton or Yale

00:29:19.300 --> 00:29:22.020
or Williams or Amherst
if it were an opportunity

00:29:22.020 --> 00:29:23.660
to women who wanted to go

00:29:23.660 --> 00:29:26.650
to an elite private nonprofit institution

00:29:26.650 --> 00:29:30.370
with wonderful faculty,
but that had a history

00:29:30.370 --> 00:29:32.520
of being a woman's college.

00:29:32.520 --> 00:29:34.660
And wanted to be at an institution

00:29:34.660 --> 00:29:39.493
that was less masculine in a way.

00:29:40.410 --> 00:29:42.810
So the pool changed to women

00:29:42.810 --> 00:29:47.500
who were more self-selecting
to go to Vassar.

00:29:47.500 --> 00:29:48.910
The male students that were coming,

00:29:48.910 --> 00:29:51.250
I would say were similar in the same way.

00:29:51.250 --> 00:29:53.850
They were making a decision
that they wanted to go

00:29:53.850 --> 00:29:57.390
to an institution that
was not as masculine

00:29:57.390 --> 00:29:58.610
as some other institutions

00:29:58.610 --> 00:30:00.700
now that this opportunity was available.

00:30:00.700 --> 00:30:04.593
And they fell into a variety
of I think different camps.

00:30:05.500 --> 00:30:07.550
We had a longtime admissions director

00:30:07.550 --> 00:30:10.040
who was really a wonderful person

00:30:10.040 --> 00:30:11.300
who was there for quite a long time.

00:30:11.300 --> 00:30:14.200
He's passed away, so I feel
okay telling a story about him

00:30:15.160 --> 00:30:18.560
but he talked about that the
applicant pool of young men

00:30:18.560 --> 00:30:22.140
often being young men who
you know had been bullied

00:30:22.140 --> 00:30:27.140
in high school by other men
or young boys at that point

00:30:28.658 --> 00:30:31.258
and wanted in an environment
that was not like that.

00:30:32.270 --> 00:30:34.960
And so I think we ended up
with a much more selective

00:30:34.960 --> 00:30:37.560
applicant pool both on the
on the part of men and women

00:30:37.560 --> 00:30:41.340
who wanted the kind of
environment that a college

00:30:41.340 --> 00:30:45.700
that had had 150 years of educating women

00:30:45.700 --> 00:30:47.590
was gonna be a comfortable environment.

00:30:47.590 --> 00:30:50.150
- Which suggests that
there's something in the DNA

00:30:50.150 --> 00:30:54.020
that carries on from the the history

00:30:54.020 --> 00:30:56.520
of having been in all women's college

00:30:57.420 --> 00:31:01.410
and that might also suggest that the same

00:31:01.410 --> 00:31:06.410
thing happens in a college that
goes co-ed and admits women.

00:31:07.660 --> 00:31:10.700
I think we've been
talking about going co-ed

00:31:10.700 --> 00:31:13.700
as if it's an arrival or a destination

00:31:13.700 --> 00:31:15.690
but in fact it's a process.

00:31:15.690 --> 00:31:20.690
So I'd like to turn to Kim
and Marvin to ask about,

00:31:22.870 --> 00:31:27.870
is that DNA still at Yale
in some sort of palpable way

00:31:28.030 --> 00:31:32.350
in terms of the unfinished
business of co-education

00:31:32.350 --> 00:31:37.350
or is there a sense of
that for students today?

00:31:37.517 --> 00:31:39.017
- You wanna start.

00:31:41.000 --> 00:31:44.050
- Good afternoon, thank
you all for coming.

00:31:44.050 --> 00:31:47.910
I really enjoyed the
sessions yesterday and today

00:31:47.910 --> 00:31:50.040
especially the one this morning.

00:31:50.040 --> 00:31:52.750
It's very inspiring for
me and my role as Dean

00:31:52.750 --> 00:31:56.260
to hear about our histories
and to see, you know,

00:31:56.260 --> 00:31:59.500
your adventurous and your resilient selves

00:31:59.500 --> 00:32:03.410
really forging a path for
Yale to be what it is today

00:32:03.410 --> 00:32:06.750
which I do think is a
really outstanding place

00:32:06.750 --> 00:32:09.093
for educating all our students.

00:32:10.230 --> 00:32:12.480
I'm looking at my name card over here

00:32:12.480 --> 00:32:14.500
and I look very undereducated.

00:32:14.500 --> 00:32:17.780
Everyone else has all
these fancy credentials

00:32:17.780 --> 00:32:18.780
but I wanted to point that out

00:32:18.780 --> 00:32:21.280
because there is one
credential that's missing here

00:32:21.280 --> 00:32:23.130
which is that I'm a parent.

00:32:23.130 --> 00:32:27.460
My daughter is a junior
here and so I get to see

00:32:27.460 --> 00:32:31.401
what an incredible place
Yale is through her eyes.

00:32:31.401 --> 00:32:34.568
(audience applauding)

00:32:35.627 --> 00:32:39.377
And you know for me the
value of co-education

00:32:40.850 --> 00:32:45.490
really just rests on the
fact that if our students,

00:32:45.490 --> 00:32:47.910
if our faculty, if we as a community,

00:32:47.910 --> 00:32:50.110
if our mission is to serve the world,

00:32:50.110 --> 00:32:52.790
then we really need to
look like the world.

00:32:52.790 --> 00:32:54.720
And it kind of really
everything really boils down

00:32:54.720 --> 00:32:57.673
to that very simple fact.

00:32:58.751 --> 00:33:01.690
And with regards to unfinished work,

00:33:01.690 --> 00:33:04.950
I think of course we've
made amazing progress

00:33:04.950 --> 00:33:08.550
and Yale now is very very
different from the Yale

00:33:08.550 --> 00:33:11.950
I've been hearing about
over the past two days.

00:33:11.950 --> 00:33:16.420
But there's still work
to do, I think one area

00:33:16.420 --> 00:33:21.420
is that we do need more
diversity in our faculty, right,

00:33:23.040 --> 00:33:25.510
it came up in discussion yesterday

00:33:25.510 --> 00:33:30.450
that 32% of our latter
faculty in the Faculty of Arts

00:33:30.450 --> 00:33:35.330
and Sciences are women
which is great progress,

00:33:35.330 --> 00:33:38.830
it's as high as it's
ever been but of course

00:33:38.830 --> 00:33:41.860
it's still quite distant from 50%.

00:33:41.860 --> 00:33:46.120
But there's a aspect of those
data that's very promising

00:33:46.120 --> 00:33:47.900
and Dean Tamar Gendler here

00:33:47.900 --> 00:33:50.090
can say more about it perhaps.

00:33:50.090 --> 00:33:52.260
But a very promising aspect of those data

00:33:52.260 --> 00:33:56.530
is that it's 32% because
that's the entire faculty,

00:33:56.530 --> 00:34:00.050
you know ranging from
recently PhD to people

00:34:00.050 --> 00:34:03.040
who've been here for 30 40 years.

00:34:03.040 --> 00:34:06.100
If you just look at the
cohort of new faculty

00:34:06.100 --> 00:34:08.030
who arrived here in the past 10 years,

00:34:08.030 --> 00:34:13.030
45% of those of the new faculty are women.

00:34:13.600 --> 00:34:15.730
So I think the trends
are looking very good,

00:34:15.730 --> 00:34:18.220
thanks to the good
efforts of our president

00:34:18.220 --> 00:34:19.153
and Dean Gendler.

00:34:20.070 --> 00:34:24.000
And I think this is very
important for our student body

00:34:24.000 --> 00:34:28.570
which of course has parity
to have a faculty that looks,

00:34:28.570 --> 00:34:31.920
that represents what
the students are like.

00:34:31.920 --> 00:34:36.270
In terms of another challenge
for undergraduate students,

00:34:36.270 --> 00:34:40.543
47% of our STEM students are women.

00:34:41.440 --> 00:34:43.340
So that's, you know there's (mumbles).

00:34:44.180 --> 00:34:47.347
(audience applauding)

00:34:48.310 --> 00:34:52.310
But you may know that still
women representation in STEM

00:34:52.310 --> 00:34:55.650
is low around the country
and this is a number

00:34:55.650 --> 00:34:59.010
that we have to keep stay focused on.

00:34:59.010 --> 00:35:03.020
There are still some fields
where the proportion of women

00:35:03.020 --> 00:35:06.520
are lower than 47% and that's been,

00:35:06.520 --> 00:35:10.040
that's an important
priority for me to raise

00:35:10.040 --> 00:35:11.400
in my role as dean.

00:35:11.400 --> 00:35:13.410
So I think I'll pause there.

00:35:13.410 --> 00:35:16.527
- So first of all, I did
graduate 10 years after you

00:35:16.527 --> 00:35:18.770
and so it's actually
wonderful to be able to see

00:35:18.770 --> 00:35:22.510
the shoulders that I'm
standing on in person.

00:35:22.510 --> 00:35:24.900
So it's been really
wonderful to hear your voices

00:35:24.900 --> 00:35:27.510
and to spend all the day with you to hear

00:35:27.510 --> 00:35:29.360
some more about your stories.

00:35:29.360 --> 00:35:32.210
I also have the privilege
of being secretary

00:35:32.210 --> 00:35:34.860
and also vice president
for student life here

00:35:34.860 --> 00:35:36.600
and that means that I'm very focused

00:35:36.600 --> 00:35:39.960
on the student experience
largely outside of the classroom,

00:35:39.960 --> 00:35:42.150
so I like to say that I
focus on where students eat,

00:35:42.150 --> 00:35:44.394
sleep, play, pray and pay.

00:35:44.394 --> 00:35:45.227
(panel laughing)

00:35:45.227 --> 00:35:46.480
Anything outside the classroom is fine

00:35:47.387 --> 00:35:49.120
and I think about that for all students,

00:35:49.120 --> 00:35:52.170
not just the college but
all students on campus.

00:35:52.170 --> 00:35:53.990
But having been a member of the college

00:35:53.990 --> 00:35:55.040
both in the dean's office

00:35:55.040 --> 00:35:57.163
and also I worked for Dick actually,

00:35:58.440 --> 00:36:00.960
and also an undergrad I do have
a special place in my heart

00:36:00.960 --> 00:36:02.520
for the undergraduates that I work with

00:36:02.520 --> 00:36:04.160
and the leadership that I work with.

00:36:04.160 --> 00:36:05.640
I think that what we would say to you

00:36:05.640 --> 00:36:09.460
in terms of, and now I'm
channeling a lot of their voices,

00:36:09.460 --> 00:36:11.780
is that yes, co-education has worked

00:36:11.780 --> 00:36:14.190
in terms of the diversity,
in terms of the number

00:36:14.190 --> 00:36:17.420
but we are all focused
on issues of equity,

00:36:17.420 --> 00:36:18.960
inclusion and belonging.

00:36:18.960 --> 00:36:21.330
And what that means is that we are focused

00:36:21.330 --> 00:36:23.810
on making sure that all of our students

00:36:23.810 --> 00:36:26.290
have equal opportunity here in ways

00:36:26.290 --> 00:36:30.353
that focus on the legal
definition of legal equity

00:36:31.870 --> 00:36:33.810
but also that they feel included,

00:36:33.810 --> 00:36:37.380
that they always feel as
if they have opportunities

00:36:37.380 --> 00:36:39.620
that are meaningful for
them and that they belong

00:36:39.620 --> 00:36:41.450
which means they can be
their authentic self.

00:36:41.450 --> 00:36:43.730
And so a lot of our student leaders,

00:36:43.730 --> 00:36:45.330
particularly women leaders are focused

00:36:45.330 --> 00:36:47.960
on those aspects of what
it means to be at Yale

00:36:47.960 --> 00:36:50.010
and feel that although
we have made progress

00:36:50.010 --> 00:36:52.330
that's some of the areas that
we really need to focus on.

00:36:52.330 --> 00:36:54.110
I think of Title Nine as
being one of the issues

00:36:54.110 --> 00:36:55.728
that many of our students,
and we have to name that

00:36:55.728 --> 00:36:56.750
because that is something very much

00:36:56.750 --> 00:36:57.920
in our students minds right now

00:36:57.920 --> 00:37:00.720
in terms of the campus
climate which has improved

00:37:00.720 --> 00:37:03.830
and which we are all stealing
still working on including.

00:37:03.830 --> 00:37:06.300
I think of very much
this group of young women

00:37:06.300 --> 00:37:08.680
being multi hyphenates and intersectional,

00:37:08.680 --> 00:37:12.020
that they are very much
focused on gender and,

00:37:12.020 --> 00:37:12.970
'cause they always think of themselves

00:37:12.970 --> 00:37:15.980
as being multiple and
holistic in their view.

00:37:15.980 --> 00:37:17.880
And so what does that mean?

00:37:17.880 --> 00:37:19.513
In fact just this week they talked to me,

00:37:19.513 --> 00:37:22.350
that women leaders talk
to me about making sure

00:37:22.350 --> 00:37:24.810
they could see themselves
in a variety of ways

00:37:24.810 --> 00:37:26.910
and not just as women.

00:37:26.910 --> 00:37:28.990
So we can have more
conversation about that

00:37:28.990 --> 00:37:31.810
but I think the demographic piece

00:37:31.810 --> 00:37:33.620
we have done well at Yale College

00:37:33.620 --> 00:37:35.630
but I think it's the inclusion
of more the belonging piece

00:37:35.630 --> 00:37:37.800
that we are now all focused
on as student leaders

00:37:37.800 --> 00:37:39.950
and the rest of us in terms of making this

00:37:39.950 --> 00:37:42.000
the best experience for these young women

00:37:42.954 --> 00:37:44.390
we have here.

00:37:44.390 --> 00:37:47.330
- Great, there are two
directions I'd love to go

00:37:47.330 --> 00:37:50.870
in relation to what you both have said.

00:37:50.870 --> 00:37:55.690
One is actually to follow
up on your experience

00:37:55.690 --> 00:38:00.690
Marvin as a parent and
the sense of the climate

00:38:00.860 --> 00:38:02.730
with the daughter here.

00:38:02.730 --> 00:38:04.430
And I've been talking to people

00:38:04.430 --> 00:38:07.070
and that's something we
haven't really talked about

00:38:07.070 --> 00:38:12.070
is the experience of the
children of our class

00:38:12.520 --> 00:38:17.260
and whether that's, you
know, how they experience.

00:38:17.260 --> 00:38:20.380
And now I guess I am asking
you to speak for your daughter

00:38:20.380 --> 00:38:23.500
and I was asking people not
to speak for someone else.

00:38:23.500 --> 00:38:28.500
But I'm interested in that
and maybe from the audience,

00:38:30.290 --> 00:38:33.870
if there's any comment
about the kind of climate

00:38:33.870 --> 00:38:37.960
or in the DNA of a school, its history

00:38:37.960 --> 00:38:42.960
or have your daughters or
sons felt a very different,

00:38:44.730 --> 00:38:46.580
obviously a very different environment.

00:38:46.580 --> 00:38:50.250
I mean there's no question
but anybody wanna...

00:38:52.110 --> 00:38:53.973
Yeah, Susan.

00:38:57.840 --> 00:39:01.000
- I'm Susan Waisbren from class of '71.

00:39:01.000 --> 00:39:04.740
I have two daughters who were
in graduate programs here

00:39:04.740 --> 00:39:06.790
in the Art History program
and then a resident

00:39:06.790 --> 00:39:08.340
at the medical school.

00:39:08.340 --> 00:39:12.820
And what struck me is
something that I struggled with

00:39:12.820 --> 00:39:16.410
when I was here which was
I wanted the opportunities

00:39:16.410 --> 00:39:20.120
of being a man but I didn't
wanna live my life as a man

00:39:20.120 --> 00:39:22.100
in terms of my career.

00:39:22.100 --> 00:39:26.660
And watching my daughters now
who are young mothers as well,

00:39:26.660 --> 00:39:30.250
they're being crushed by the
amount of responsibilities

00:39:30.250 --> 00:39:31.330
that they have.

00:39:31.330 --> 00:39:34.990
And I say to them, now
that you're 50% doctors,

00:39:34.990 --> 00:39:38.990
now that you're, you know,
50% almost professors,

00:39:38.990 --> 00:39:42.750
you need to stand up for
how can a woman be a mother

00:39:42.750 --> 00:39:47.290
and also her career and not
have four overnights a week

00:39:47.290 --> 00:39:52.290
(mumbles).
(audience applauding)

00:39:52.960 --> 00:39:56.320
- So the advantages and
some of the tribulations

00:39:56.320 --> 00:40:00.640
that come from those opportunities.

00:40:00.640 --> 00:40:05.410
I don't know Marvin if you
wanna say anything else

00:40:05.410 --> 00:40:07.857
about the next generation (mumbles).

00:40:08.850 --> 00:40:12.783
- So over 50 years and in fact
even in the past 10 years,

00:40:14.980 --> 00:40:17.700
we continue to evolve and try to make this

00:40:17.700 --> 00:40:18.900
a more inclusive climate.

00:40:18.900 --> 00:40:22.410
One area that seems now very natural

00:40:22.410 --> 00:40:25.170
but definitely it did
not exist 10 years ago

00:40:25.170 --> 00:40:27.230
is that our housing is gender-neutral now.

00:40:27.230 --> 00:40:31.150
And so the bathrooms have
always been gender neutral,

00:40:31.150 --> 00:40:34.840
I mean or unshared but the housing,

00:40:34.840 --> 00:40:37.830
the configurations themselves
have gone gender-neutral

00:40:37.830 --> 00:40:40.950
over the past 10 years recently extended

00:40:40.950 --> 00:40:43.090
to first-year students as well.

00:40:43.090 --> 00:40:45.630
So we continue to evolve in that sense.

00:40:45.630 --> 00:40:47.370
I'm gonna choose not to
represent my daughter

00:40:47.370 --> 00:40:48.880
because she doesn't like that

00:40:48.880 --> 00:40:53.880
but in general speaking again
with my administrative hat,

00:40:54.070 --> 00:40:56.290
I think progress continues to be made.

00:40:56.290 --> 00:40:58.673
Challenges in areas where
we still need to work on,

00:40:58.673 --> 00:41:01.450
they're still gendered,
they're still exist

00:41:01.450 --> 00:41:03.513
gendered social spaces.

00:41:05.300 --> 00:41:07.450
On the bright side organizations,

00:41:07.450 --> 00:41:09.980
groups like that where improves
have gone co-ed last year

00:41:09.980 --> 00:41:12.240
as I'm sure many of you are aware

00:41:12.240 --> 00:41:13.570
but there are still many singing groups

00:41:13.570 --> 00:41:16.850
that are single sex.

00:41:16.850 --> 00:41:20.930
There are still Greek
organizations that are single sex.

00:41:20.930 --> 00:41:25.443
So these are areas that we
have to continue to attend to.

00:41:27.450 --> 00:41:31.010
- And I would say I think
going to your specific story

00:41:31.010 --> 00:41:33.350
is what you see a lot in
this particular generation

00:41:33.350 --> 00:41:36.653
of young women in the
college is conversations

00:41:36.653 --> 00:41:39.240
about balance and wellness.

00:41:39.240 --> 00:41:44.240
So as a generation, they
have really been focusing

00:41:44.710 --> 00:41:48.790
on how best to manage their
time and their energy.

00:41:48.790 --> 00:41:51.100
They are also talking about the concept

00:41:51.100 --> 00:41:54.510
of effortless perfection
and trying to sort of kill

00:41:54.510 --> 00:41:55.810
that dragon at the same time.

00:41:55.810 --> 00:41:58.660
So we spent a lot of
time developing programs

00:41:58.660 --> 00:41:59.830
that address that issue.

00:41:59.830 --> 00:42:03.810
In fact Laurie Santos had a
course a couple of years ago

00:42:03.810 --> 00:42:05.990
called The Psychology of The Good Life

00:42:05.990 --> 00:42:08.100
but we have built The Good Life Center

00:42:08.100 --> 00:42:10.240
in so many colleges and we
have a chance to look at it

00:42:10.240 --> 00:42:13.650
which basically offers
classes that help you

00:42:13.650 --> 00:42:15.710
make a decision about how
you're gonna manage your time

00:42:15.710 --> 00:42:17.337
and your energy but also
how do you manage your life

00:42:17.337 --> 00:42:18.890
and how do you think things through.

00:42:18.890 --> 00:42:20.360
So there's ways in which this generation

00:42:20.360 --> 00:42:23.320
is just much more focused
on making sure certainly,

00:42:23.320 --> 00:42:24.517
I'm talking about the college now

00:42:24.517 --> 00:42:27.080
and your students are a
little bit older than them.

00:42:27.080 --> 00:42:29.710
But the college, the young
women are much more focused

00:42:29.710 --> 00:42:32.160
on trying to make sure
they have a balanced life.

00:42:33.030 --> 00:42:36.240
- And it also raises the
question of professions.

00:42:36.240 --> 00:42:37.073
- Yes.

00:42:37.073 --> 00:42:41.550
- And coming, being at
Yale as a graduate student,

00:42:41.550 --> 00:42:46.040
I mentioned, so you were one
of two Cathy in economics.

00:42:46.040 --> 00:42:50.730
But how do we need to see our discussion

00:42:50.730 --> 00:42:53.910
of undergraduate
co-education within a lens

00:42:53.910 --> 00:42:56.660
that includes professions that women enter

00:42:56.660 --> 00:43:01.660
and the different times,
the different time periods

00:43:01.750 --> 00:43:05.080
where various fields admitted women

00:43:05.080 --> 00:43:09.480
had more women, what about (mumbles)--

00:43:09.480 --> 00:43:13.470
- I'm hoping will continue to
see things evolve over time

00:43:13.470 --> 00:43:16.770
and I think as we have more
women faculty coming up

00:43:16.770 --> 00:43:19.400
through the ranks as we
have more women lawyers,

00:43:19.400 --> 00:43:23.540
more women doctors that that
they will start thinking

00:43:23.540 --> 00:43:26.020
about the workplace and
making sure that it's a place

00:43:26.020 --> 00:43:29.240
that's comfortable for
not just women but men

00:43:29.240 --> 00:43:33.110
as they try to have lives
outside of their professions.

00:43:33.110 --> 00:43:36.170
I'm seeing amongst the
(mumbles) three kids

00:43:36.170 --> 00:43:38.750
who are all in a not quite there yet

00:43:38.750 --> 00:43:43.320
but thinking about these
issues both two boys and a girl

00:43:43.320 --> 00:43:47.580
and they care about
balance, all of them do.

00:43:47.580 --> 00:43:50.780
And so I think this
generation is gonna bring

00:43:50.780 --> 00:43:53.280
those values into the workplace

00:43:53.280 --> 00:43:54.630
and that's what we need them to do

00:43:54.630 --> 00:43:57.640
to make it more reasonable
for both men and women

00:43:57.640 --> 00:44:01.110
to lead lives that work as well.

00:44:01.110 --> 00:44:03.240
- Kim I wanna follow up on what you said

00:44:03.240 --> 00:44:07.110
about gender fluidity and a whole,

00:44:07.110 --> 00:44:10.540
I mean again the word,
the term co-education

00:44:10.540 --> 00:44:15.540
presupposes binary or mostly presupposes

00:44:17.130 --> 00:44:20.123
that and as you pointed out
on college campus today,

00:44:21.100 --> 00:44:25.190
there's a very different
discussion vocabulary

00:44:25.190 --> 00:44:30.190
and climate around sexual
identification gender,

00:44:31.070 --> 00:44:35.010
identification, how does
that, how do we need to think

00:44:35.010 --> 00:44:38.680
about that in relation to the
discussion about co-education?

00:44:38.680 --> 00:44:40.390
- Well, we think about
it certainly in terms

00:44:40.390 --> 00:44:42.217
of as Marvin mentioned,
gender-neutral bathrooms

00:44:42.217 --> 00:44:46.750
and you saw the all gender
bathrooms right down the hall.

00:44:46.750 --> 00:44:48.020
And how do you negotiate that

00:44:48.020 --> 00:44:49.290
when you have multiple generations?

00:44:49.290 --> 00:44:51.990
Some of us actually still
want men and women's bathroom,

00:44:51.990 --> 00:44:53.430
so how do you manage that with students

00:44:53.430 --> 00:44:55.150
who are looking for something different.

00:44:55.150 --> 00:44:58.280
But we also have, we're looking at things,

00:44:58.280 --> 00:45:01.540
very basic things like,
you know, how do we account

00:45:01.540 --> 00:45:03.960
for someone's gender changing gender

00:45:03.960 --> 00:45:06.240
or their gender identity on class list

00:45:06.240 --> 00:45:07.610
because they're generated very much

00:45:07.610 --> 00:45:11.680
by you know our database
and how do we help faculty

00:45:11.680 --> 00:45:13.220
understand who is in front of them,

00:45:13.220 --> 00:45:15.610
how do we help faculty
understand the person

00:45:15.610 --> 00:45:17.220
whose name that might be legally Michael

00:45:17.220 --> 00:45:18.390
might be Michelle at some point.

00:45:18.390 --> 00:45:20.630
So we've got systems
in place we've designed

00:45:20.630 --> 00:45:23.783
to make sure that an accurate identity

00:45:23.783 --> 00:45:25.550
is in front of the faculty members.

00:45:25.550 --> 00:45:27.450
And we are actually
doing a lot of training

00:45:27.450 --> 00:45:29.090
with faculty and staff and students

00:45:29.090 --> 00:45:33.850
about transgender students
which is a very big component

00:45:33.850 --> 00:45:36.850
of the population of
student issues on campus

00:45:36.850 --> 00:45:38.620
on across the country.

00:45:38.620 --> 00:45:40.940
So we're always thinking about gender

00:45:40.940 --> 00:45:42.920
broader than just male-female.

00:45:42.920 --> 00:45:45.890
Even in my reports that
I get from my staff

00:45:45.890 --> 00:45:47.410
about you know who's in the room

00:45:47.410 --> 00:45:50.150
and they give me a list
of students I might meet.

00:45:50.150 --> 00:45:51.497
Well, they'll say something like,

00:45:51.497 --> 00:45:55.130
"Well, we have four people
presenting as male."

00:45:55.130 --> 00:45:56.990
So even our language has shifted

00:45:56.990 --> 00:45:58.400
in terms of how we address the issue

00:45:58.400 --> 00:46:01.850
but it certainly our
policies, our programs,

00:46:01.850 --> 00:46:02.690
even though some of our billings

00:46:02.690 --> 00:46:03.710
are sort of addressing that.

00:46:03.710 --> 00:46:05.180
So we've got to be much broader

00:46:05.180 --> 00:46:07.453
in terms of how we think
about these issues.

00:46:08.910 --> 00:46:12.350
- Let me throw that open
to anyone else on the panel

00:46:13.500 --> 00:46:18.500
to talk about intersectionalities
and fluidity of gender

00:46:18.820 --> 00:46:22.170
and just the way of thinking about

00:46:23.950 --> 00:46:27.417
who comes to the college and inclusion

00:46:27.417 --> 00:46:29.043
and how they feel included.

00:46:30.900 --> 00:46:31.890
- Anybody else?

00:46:31.890 --> 00:46:35.680
- Intersectionality and
inclusion are inter-acting

00:46:35.680 --> 00:46:39.070
and overlapping but by no
means identical issues.

00:46:39.070 --> 00:46:42.850
It seems to me Cathy is one
of the most famous people

00:46:42.850 --> 00:46:44.570
in the country associated with the idea

00:46:44.570 --> 00:46:46.650
of promoting inclusion.

00:46:46.650 --> 00:46:49.129
And I'd have to say
anybody who'd say nowadays,

00:46:49.129 --> 00:46:52.700
you know, was co-education
over, was it accomplished

00:46:52.700 --> 00:46:55.540
when women took up their
places as students?

00:46:55.540 --> 00:46:57.230
Yes and no, right.

00:46:57.230 --> 00:46:58.970
It was and it wasn't.

00:46:58.970 --> 00:47:00.610
It was because they were here,

00:47:00.610 --> 00:47:03.110
it wasn't because Yale needed to be remade

00:47:03.110 --> 00:47:06.620
to become the place
they would be fully able

00:47:06.620 --> 00:47:08.800
to live up to their highest potential in.

00:47:08.800 --> 00:47:10.870
But I think anybody who
lived here at the time

00:47:10.870 --> 00:47:14.700
realizes that the deepest
thing co-education did to Yale

00:47:14.700 --> 00:47:17.140
is to induce the habit every so often

00:47:17.140 --> 00:47:20.060
of looking at the university
and asking yourself,

00:47:20.060 --> 00:47:22.898
I know it's great but is it really,

00:47:22.898 --> 00:47:26.260
does this place really serve the mission

00:47:26.260 --> 00:47:27.860
it pushed forward in the world?

00:47:27.860 --> 00:47:30.560
And at a certain point, Yale
couldn't answer that question

00:47:30.560 --> 00:47:31.700
when it excluded women.

00:47:31.700 --> 00:47:34.020
And so women were included,
certainly it happened

00:47:34.020 --> 00:47:36.110
the same with the question
of race in a significant way

00:47:36.110 --> 00:47:38.500
also in the late 60s and early 70s.

00:47:38.500 --> 00:47:41.300
And you know now of course we
are all much more preoccupied

00:47:41.300 --> 00:47:43.720
with socioeconomic
questions now that we know

00:47:43.720 --> 00:47:47.440
the very high correlation
between family income level

00:47:49.020 --> 00:47:51.673
from birth to going to
college preparation,

00:47:52.543 --> 00:47:55.070
over preparation, grooming that people get

00:47:55.070 --> 00:47:56.480
and how those correlate.

00:47:56.480 --> 00:47:57.810
And so I think that's really,

00:47:57.810 --> 00:48:00.490
I think as someone who was
but until very recently,

00:48:00.490 --> 00:48:02.300
a university president, I would say,

00:48:02.300 --> 00:48:05.010
that's been the new horizon,
the main new horizon

00:48:06.070 --> 00:48:07.940
that co-education opened up for us.

00:48:07.940 --> 00:48:10.640
And the thing is even when that is solved

00:48:10.640 --> 00:48:13.310
to our satisfaction, I
hope after about five years

00:48:13.310 --> 00:48:16.480
people will say, all right,
we've reached the stage

00:48:16.480 --> 00:48:18.070
of self-satisfaction again,

00:48:18.070 --> 00:48:19.530
now let's ask the question again,

00:48:19.530 --> 00:48:21.940
is this place actually
open to all the people

00:48:21.940 --> 00:48:23.973
it has a mission to serve?

00:48:25.460 --> 00:48:28.410
- Yeah, I think just one
of the real challenges

00:48:28.410 --> 00:48:31.790
on college campuses is getting students

00:48:31.790 --> 00:48:36.360
to listen to each other
and as our communities

00:48:36.360 --> 00:48:38.870
become significantly more diverse

00:48:38.870 --> 00:48:42.010
just one of the real advantages
of being here for four years

00:48:42.010 --> 00:48:45.030
is to learn from people that
have had different experiences.

00:48:45.030 --> 00:48:47.296
And sometimes I think there's a tendency

00:48:47.296 --> 00:48:52.296
to not let that happen
and to choose one identity

00:48:52.550 --> 00:48:55.730
and feel like you need
to somehow defend it.

00:48:55.730 --> 00:48:58.770
And I think certainly my time at Vassar

00:48:58.770 --> 00:49:00.780
was reminding students to take advantage

00:49:00.780 --> 00:49:03.400
of the amazing diversity of the campus

00:49:03.400 --> 00:49:07.300
and not only speak up
but listen to their peers

00:49:07.300 --> 00:49:09.163
about their experiences as well.

00:49:10.292 --> 00:49:13.723
- All right, I wanna open
it up now for questions

00:49:14.620 --> 00:49:16.250
for the remainder of our time.

00:49:16.250 --> 00:49:20.950
So we have about 20 minutes for questions.

00:49:20.950 --> 00:49:21.783
Yeah.

00:49:27.440 --> 00:49:32.440
- Hello, I'm Susan Trina
Quay, 1971 Calhoun.

00:49:32.650 --> 00:49:36.190
I wonder whether any
thoughts been given at Yale

00:49:36.190 --> 00:49:38.432
in addressing co-education values

00:49:38.432 --> 00:49:42.453
through modifications
of traditional pedagogy.

00:49:43.760 --> 00:49:48.070
We know that co-ed and
all-male classrooms,

00:49:48.070 --> 00:49:51.940
the dynamics are quite different
from all-female classrooms.

00:49:51.940 --> 00:49:54.510
We also know that female instructors

00:49:54.510 --> 00:49:58.010
are challenged much more
frequently both by both male

00:49:58.010 --> 00:50:01.930
and female students that
are male instructors.

00:50:01.930 --> 00:50:05.490
And finally we know that the
traditional pedagogical model

00:50:05.490 --> 00:50:09.570
at Yale, the sage on the
stage, well usually a guy

00:50:09.570 --> 00:50:11.510
in a tweed jacket, something like that,

00:50:11.510 --> 00:50:16.240
maybe a woman who is following that model

00:50:16.240 --> 00:50:19.240
maybe suits a kind of masculine culture

00:50:19.240 --> 00:50:21.130
but may not bring out the strength

00:50:21.130 --> 00:50:25.440
and a feminine culture
or something in between.

00:50:25.440 --> 00:50:29.840
So my question, I think
going beyond the question

00:50:29.840 --> 00:50:34.840
of how to retain the
best women instructors

00:50:35.550 --> 00:50:39.050
to build a truly diverse faculty,

00:50:39.050 --> 00:50:43.360
what is being done to
examine pedagogical models

00:50:43.360 --> 00:50:45.080
that are popular at Yale?

00:50:45.080 --> 00:50:48.440
I'll only say this, and this
may be too ancient history,

00:50:48.440 --> 00:50:51.040
I don't know they remember
the old joke I heard

00:50:51.040 --> 00:50:54.190
when I came to Yale, how
many Yale's does it take

00:50:54.190 --> 00:50:55.783
to screw in a light bulb?

00:50:57.110 --> 00:50:59.960
Want to screw it in and
one to kick out the stool

00:50:59.960 --> 00:51:02.113
underneath him while he's trying to do it.

00:51:03.000 --> 00:51:05.390
When I came to Yale, I was really stunned

00:51:05.390 --> 00:51:08.250
at the level of brutal competition,

00:51:08.250 --> 00:51:11.270
finding pages and books razored out

00:51:11.270 --> 00:51:14.670
that have been left on reserve
in cross campus and so on

00:51:14.670 --> 00:51:18.920
and that there were no rules
when it came to winning.

00:51:18.920 --> 00:51:21.630
I've assumed that's moderated over time

00:51:21.630 --> 00:51:26.630
but the kind of valorisation
of that kind of competition

00:51:27.310 --> 00:51:32.300
and the valorisation of a
highly authoritative professor,

00:51:32.300 --> 00:51:35.890
I think speaks to a kind of DNA at Yale

00:51:35.890 --> 00:51:38.090
that may need some re-examination.

00:51:38.090 --> 00:51:39.930
So what do you think?

00:51:39.930 --> 00:51:42.580
- I wonder if I could pitch into this.

00:51:42.580 --> 00:51:43.930
You've just described a Yale,

00:51:43.930 --> 00:51:46.111
I've been here all that time, right,

00:51:46.111 --> 00:51:49.321
until I was here for 40 years
for my first freshman day

00:51:49.321 --> 00:51:51.330
until I left the faculty.

00:51:51.330 --> 00:51:54.920
The Yale you describe of
the man knocking the stool

00:51:54.920 --> 00:51:57.140
out from another it's not one I ever knew.

00:51:57.140 --> 00:52:00.388
I never experienced that kind
that kind of competitiveness

00:52:00.388 --> 00:52:04.460
at this place.
(audience applauding)

00:52:04.460 --> 00:52:06.350
I think if women had come to a place

00:52:06.350 --> 00:52:08.410
that had that culture it
would have been very different

00:52:08.410 --> 00:52:11.440
and I bet even that would have
had a modifying force in it.

00:52:11.440 --> 00:52:13.940
But don't forget as for the
model of pedagogy, you know,

00:52:13.940 --> 00:52:16.270
let me just say, I was a
department chair, I was a Dean,

00:52:16.270 --> 00:52:17.930
the greatest lecturers in my department

00:52:17.930 --> 00:52:20.690
included both men and
women, the greatest seminar,

00:52:20.690 --> 00:52:22.272
Susie Wofford, is in this room.

00:52:22.272 --> 00:52:26.128
Your classmate who was
such a great one then,

00:52:26.128 --> 00:52:30.560
Tamar Gendler, a great one
of more modern vintage.

00:52:30.560 --> 00:52:32.370
But the great majority
of you have instruction

00:52:32.370 --> 00:52:33.623
is done in small groups.

00:52:34.542 --> 00:52:38.360
And where it does not, in
small groups never work

00:52:38.360 --> 00:52:42.880
if it's all me me me on
the part of the faculty.

00:52:42.880 --> 00:52:45.440
Finding a way to be gifted
at seminar instruction

00:52:45.440 --> 00:52:47.780
is one of the really
powerful things universities

00:52:47.780 --> 00:52:49.480
teach students 'cause it has to do,

00:52:49.480 --> 00:52:51.430
how do you find an interesting question

00:52:51.430 --> 00:52:52.960
and then how do you create the space

00:52:52.960 --> 00:52:56.270
where different people can
hazard some portion of the answer

00:52:56.270 --> 00:52:57.310
and learn from each other?

00:52:57.310 --> 00:52:59.650
That's a great thing
universities teach people.

00:52:59.650 --> 00:53:02.260
And it seems to me the small group culture

00:53:02.260 --> 00:53:05.540
was strong from my earliest
days on the faculty

00:53:05.540 --> 00:53:07.260
and I believe is probably
even stronger now.

00:53:07.260 --> 00:53:09.330
- I also think there's a lot more emphasis

00:53:09.330 --> 00:53:12.730
on helping faculty think about pedagogy

00:53:12.730 --> 00:53:15.770
so that their classroom can be supportive

00:53:15.770 --> 00:53:17.600
for everybody that's around the table.

00:53:17.600 --> 00:53:19.420
That that was not something
that you've learned

00:53:19.420 --> 00:53:21.760
in graduate school when I was here

00:53:21.760 --> 00:53:24.100
but now it is something that institutions

00:53:24.100 --> 00:53:25.300
are much more conscious of.

00:53:25.300 --> 00:53:27.920
And I just wanna say there were 30 people

00:53:27.920 --> 00:53:31.150
in the Econ PhD program
the year I came, two women,

00:53:31.150 --> 00:53:33.190
it was incredibly supportive.

00:53:33.190 --> 00:53:35.350
And I loved every minute of it.

00:53:35.350 --> 00:53:38.240
So I also did not experience that.

00:53:38.240 --> 00:53:41.410
And other PhD programs
had other reputations

00:53:41.410 --> 00:53:43.540
and so part of the
reason for coming to Yale

00:53:43.540 --> 00:53:46.840
was precisely because it did
not have that reputation.

00:53:46.840 --> 00:53:49.153
- And I just have to
add and dump on there,

00:53:50.400 --> 00:53:53.490
I think we're very fortunate
that our reputation

00:53:53.490 --> 00:53:56.520
in higher education especially
amongst this generation

00:53:56.520 --> 00:53:59.580
of students is that we
are a strong community,

00:53:59.580 --> 00:54:00.970
we are a collaborative community,

00:54:00.970 --> 00:54:02.600
we are supportive of each other.

00:54:02.600 --> 00:54:04.560
So this competitive notion,

00:54:04.560 --> 00:54:06.970
I think really lives in the past.

00:54:06.970 --> 00:54:10.450
I also wanna add that
we've done, we do surveys,

00:54:10.450 --> 00:54:15.450
not survey, sorry, we analyze
the effectiveness of teachers

00:54:15.790 --> 00:54:17.700
and how students view their teachers

00:54:17.700 --> 00:54:20.428
both for small classes and large classes.

00:54:20.428 --> 00:54:25.300
And on average, our women faculty

00:54:25.300 --> 00:54:28.010
actually received
numerically higher ratings

00:54:28.010 --> 00:54:31.950
from other students and
than the male faculty do

00:54:31.950 --> 00:54:35.490
if we break it down by gender.

00:54:35.490 --> 00:54:37.610
The second thing I'll
say is that regarding

00:54:37.610 --> 00:54:40.730
the kind of a big
authoritative large lectures,

00:54:40.730 --> 00:54:42.960
this is course by Professor Laurie Santos

00:54:42.960 --> 00:54:45.623
is the largest in Yale College history.

00:54:46.588 --> 00:54:51.588
And so I think it's
because of her charisma

00:54:51.940 --> 00:54:54.980
and the respect that
students have for who she is

00:54:54.980 --> 00:54:56.940
that has led to its size.

00:54:56.940 --> 00:55:00.690
Even this semester, the largest
class is taught by women,

00:55:00.690 --> 00:55:05.690
my wife and even though she's
going against at the same time

00:55:07.335 --> 00:55:08.610
a distinguished male professor,

00:55:08.610 --> 00:55:10.730
Akhil Amar of Constitutional Law.

00:55:10.730 --> 00:55:13.570
So I think I think the
culture is pretty good.

00:55:13.570 --> 00:55:15.380
- And also just so you know
that right across the street

00:55:15.380 --> 00:55:18.680
is the physical manifestation
of our commitment to pedagogy

00:55:18.680 --> 00:55:20.460
which is the Poorvu Center
for Teaching and Learning

00:55:20.460 --> 00:55:22.250
which does a lot of training of faculty

00:55:22.250 --> 00:55:26.450
about new ways of teaching
and experiential learning

00:55:26.450 --> 00:55:27.820
and how to deal with
people who are introverted.

00:55:27.820 --> 00:55:29.890
So all kinds of ways of
making sure the class dynamics

00:55:29.890 --> 00:55:32.230
are really rich because if they weren't,

00:55:32.230 --> 00:55:33.990
we would probably wouldn't be getting

00:55:33.990 --> 00:55:34.823
as many students as we are

00:55:34.823 --> 00:55:38.163
but that is our commitment.

00:55:39.170 --> 00:55:41.770
- Yeah, so there's been a
lot of change in the way

00:55:41.770 --> 00:55:46.770
on multiple campuses how people
are trained in pedagogical,

00:55:48.650 --> 00:55:51.020
you know and to think
about that and teaching,

00:55:51.020 --> 00:55:52.840
but I have to say I think that Yale

00:55:52.840 --> 00:55:57.460
has always been focused
on undergraduate teaching

00:55:57.460 --> 00:56:00.240
in a very real authentic way.

00:56:00.240 --> 00:56:02.930
As part of the university it's not a place

00:56:02.930 --> 00:56:05.330
where the only thing that faculty cared

00:56:05.330 --> 00:56:09.130
about when we were there
and now is research

00:56:09.130 --> 00:56:12.610
and not how that enters into the classroom

00:56:12.610 --> 00:56:14.540
and what happens among students.

00:56:14.540 --> 00:56:17.383
So, yep, back there.

00:56:20.075 --> 00:56:25.075
- (mumbles) my name is
Cate Moore, class of '73.

00:56:25.890 --> 00:56:26.823
Did you hear that?

00:56:28.040 --> 00:56:30.390
I have a question, not a speech,

00:56:30.390 --> 00:56:33.680
and I'd like to direct the
question primarily to Kimberly

00:56:33.680 --> 00:56:35.973
and to Marvin, is that right?

00:56:37.490 --> 00:56:40.600
We have the rise of the cultural centers

00:56:40.600 --> 00:56:45.210
but what mechanisms are we
actively using to promote

00:56:45.210 --> 00:56:50.210
civil discourse among the
different cultural components

00:56:51.690 --> 00:56:53.505
of the student body?

00:56:53.505 --> 00:56:56.550
(audience applauding)

00:56:56.550 --> 00:56:59.950
- That goes back to Cathy's
question as well, Kim.

00:56:59.950 --> 00:57:01.940
- So one of the things
we've been focusing on

00:57:01.940 --> 00:57:06.020
on the last year and a
half is what we call,

00:57:06.020 --> 00:57:09.720
training students on how to
have difficult conversations

00:57:09.720 --> 00:57:11.720
and this is one of the
things that actually came up

00:57:11.720 --> 00:57:13.470
from the student body.

00:57:13.470 --> 00:57:14.670
We have an advisory group of talking

00:57:14.670 --> 00:57:15.503
about what are some of the issues

00:57:15.503 --> 00:57:17.010
we should be dealing
with and this one things

00:57:17.010 --> 00:57:20.240
that they all seem to say that
we need to be focusing on,

00:57:20.240 --> 00:57:22.720
Marvin and I, and actually
mostly Marvin's gonna start

00:57:22.720 --> 00:57:24.210
working with a group of
faculty members to come up

00:57:24.210 --> 00:57:26.530
with some programming,
very specific programming

00:57:26.530 --> 00:57:28.940
including classroom,
hopefully classroom work

00:57:28.940 --> 00:57:32.730
to help students interact
differently with each other.

00:57:32.730 --> 00:57:34.310
But the students themselves are actually

00:57:34.310 --> 00:57:38.870
coming up with informal
ways of educating each other

00:57:38.870 --> 00:57:40.550
about different issues.

00:57:40.550 --> 00:57:43.100
We are actually promoting conversations

00:57:43.100 --> 00:57:45.080
with different viewpoints
on the same platform

00:57:45.080 --> 00:57:46.310
because typically what happens

00:57:46.310 --> 00:57:48.666
mostly because of it's difficult to get

00:57:48.666 --> 00:57:51.920
to pay for multiple speakers
on the same platform.

00:57:51.920 --> 00:57:53.900
We're trying to make sure that students

00:57:53.900 --> 00:57:55.290
have the money to be able to do that

00:57:55.290 --> 00:57:57.510
so they tend invite one person at a time.

00:57:57.510 --> 00:57:59.100
We would like to have three or four people

00:57:59.100 --> 00:58:00.980
off the same topic coming
from different perspective.

00:58:00.980 --> 00:58:02.400
So you'll see that a lot more.

00:58:02.400 --> 00:58:03.910
So we're paying a lot
more attention to that.

00:58:03.910 --> 00:58:04.743
So (mumbles) think you'll see more

00:58:04.743 --> 00:58:07.040
in the next couple of
years particularly given

00:58:07.040 --> 00:58:08.720
the social and the political climate,

00:58:08.720 --> 00:58:10.410
we feel it's very important to double down

00:58:10.410 --> 00:58:13.020
on those kind of skill sets for students.

00:58:13.020 --> 00:58:14.460
And so with student leadership,

00:58:14.460 --> 00:58:17.350
Marvin and I are trying
to work on that now.

00:58:17.350 --> 00:58:18.290
Thank you for your question.

00:58:18.290 --> 00:58:20.640
- Thank you and just to add a little,

00:58:20.640 --> 00:58:22.180
you know, we're fortunate here at Yale

00:58:22.180 --> 00:58:27.052
to have the most powerful
mechanism for promoting learning

00:58:27.052 --> 00:58:30.000
from each other and civil
discourse which in my view

00:58:30.000 --> 00:58:33.480
is the residential
colleges because we force--

00:58:33.480 --> 00:58:36.310
(audience applauding)

00:58:36.310 --> 00:58:39.720
Because we require
students to live together

00:58:39.720 --> 00:58:44.713
and you know for at least two
years because we mix them up,

00:58:45.780 --> 00:58:48.850
you know, you can't really choose to live

00:58:48.850 --> 00:58:51.900
and spend all your time with
people who are like yourselves.

00:58:51.900 --> 00:58:54.120
And because we put so much resources

00:58:54.120 --> 00:58:57.580
into the dining-hall
community and you know,

00:58:57.580 --> 00:59:00.780
after our evening a buttery community,

00:59:00.780 --> 00:59:03.150
students are really always
find themselves in settings

00:59:03.150 --> 00:59:06.950
where they can meet people who
are different from themselves

00:59:06.950 --> 00:59:08.820
and hear their different perspectives.

00:59:08.820 --> 00:59:11.240
And you know, and
sometimes it can be tense

00:59:11.240 --> 00:59:14.323
but at least where people
are in conversation.

00:59:14.323 --> 00:59:18.220
And so coupled with the
programs that Kim has described

00:59:18.220 --> 00:59:19.810
that we're working hard to develop.

00:59:19.810 --> 00:59:22.700
I think we are in a good
position to address this.

00:59:22.700 --> 00:59:24.550
So it is a very high priority for us.

00:59:27.152 --> 00:59:29.319
- Okay, woman right there.

00:59:36.250 --> 00:59:39.903
- Pat Williams, class of
'71, I was in Pearson.

00:59:40.890 --> 00:59:43.940
Drunken parties at Yale have gotten

00:59:43.940 --> 00:59:47.240
a lot of publicity lately and--

00:59:47.240 --> 00:59:49.500
- Which parties, sorry
could you say that again?

00:59:49.500 --> 00:59:50.333
- Drunken--

00:59:51.236 --> 00:59:52.923
(background noise drowns other sounds)

00:59:52.923 --> 00:59:54.160
- Oh, drunken parties, okay.

00:59:54.160 --> 00:59:59.160
- And I'm wondering what attention is paid

01:00:01.070 --> 01:00:04.503
to that aspect of
substance abuse on campus?

01:00:09.920 --> 01:00:12.760
- We do a lot of training for students

01:00:12.760 --> 01:00:15.290
when they initially come
in about our policies

01:00:15.290 --> 01:00:18.120
and we have a number of students on campus

01:00:18.120 --> 01:00:20.710
who they're called a
Communication Consent Corners

01:00:20.710 --> 01:00:24.570
to talk a little bit about
how the party effectively

01:00:24.570 --> 01:00:26.790
and work with student
organizations to make sure

01:00:26.790 --> 01:00:29.290
that they're doing
something appropriately,

01:00:29.290 --> 01:00:30.723
effectively meaning safely.

01:00:31.860 --> 01:00:33.667
Sometimes what we find
out is that when parties

01:00:33.667 --> 01:00:37.380
are at campus, they're actually
well done and they're safe

01:00:37.380 --> 01:00:38.920
but when students go off campus,

01:00:38.920 --> 01:00:40.440
that's where we have more problems.

01:00:40.440 --> 01:00:42.070
So we're spending a lot
more time with fraternities

01:00:42.070 --> 01:00:44.113
and sororities, mostly fraternities,

01:00:44.950 --> 01:00:46.530
to have conversation with them

01:00:46.530 --> 01:00:48.800
about what our expectation
are and monitoring them

01:00:48.800 --> 01:00:50.890
in very different ways
but we're also spending

01:00:50.890 --> 01:00:52.730
a lot of time frankly with
the students themselves

01:00:52.730 --> 01:00:54.610
to make sure they're
looking out for each other

01:00:54.610 --> 01:00:56.250
and they understand how to how to party

01:00:56.250 --> 01:00:57.083
a little bit more safely.

01:00:57.083 --> 01:00:59.310
So we haven't had as
many issues on campus.

01:00:59.310 --> 01:01:02.990
In fact, one of the innovations
that Marvin did this year

01:01:02.990 --> 01:01:06.003
was to have a huge
party the first weekend,

01:01:07.590 --> 01:01:09.720
the first weekend that the
first year's class came on,

01:01:09.720 --> 01:01:14.720
so all of campus, was it
off, was across campus,

01:01:15.150 --> 01:01:16.427
was taken over--
- Old campus.

01:01:16.427 --> 01:01:18.930
- Old campus, old campus
was taken over for something

01:01:18.930 --> 01:01:20.650
we call the Bulldog
bash which is something

01:01:20.650 --> 01:01:23.090
we've done for two years
running which is a party

01:01:23.090 --> 01:01:26.410
that we throw where the
Schwarzman Center at Marvin's team

01:01:26.410 --> 01:01:30.260
throws which has five acts
going from 9:30 to 2 p.m.,

01:01:30.260 --> 01:01:32.037
- Am.
- Through 2 a.m.,

01:01:33.140 --> 01:01:35.890
if feels like 2 p.m for those
that were actually there.

01:01:37.090 --> 01:01:40.230
And it does have a beer
garden where you have to,

01:01:40.230 --> 01:01:42.270
older students can go and
including graduate students,

01:01:42.270 --> 01:01:44.960
lots of food and basically
there's so much fun

01:01:44.960 --> 01:01:47.040
that nobody actually
ever goes away from that.

01:01:47.040 --> 01:01:49.440
So they have a experience
of having a really good

01:01:49.440 --> 01:01:51.740
that first party being really really fun,

01:01:51.740 --> 01:01:53.410
meeting people in a safe environment

01:01:53.410 --> 01:01:55.230
and having better sets of ideas

01:01:55.230 --> 01:01:58.760
about how to party more
safely other places.

01:01:58.760 --> 01:02:01.230
So we're trying to have again programming,

01:02:01.230 --> 01:02:03.810
good modeling and a lot of
conversations with students

01:02:03.810 --> 01:02:06.790
that we think might be
problematic early on.

01:02:06.790 --> 01:02:08.890
- I was a college head for nine years

01:02:10.138 --> 01:02:15.138
and I've run many events
with alcohol, beer and wine

01:02:15.830 --> 01:02:19.100
for my senior students and
I've not had a single student

01:02:19.100 --> 01:02:22.970
misbehave or get sick at any
of the events that I hosted.

01:02:22.970 --> 01:02:26.630
And what this told me was
that if we can throw a party

01:02:26.630 --> 01:02:30.250
that serves alcohol and treat
the students like adults,

01:02:30.250 --> 01:02:33.580
the young adults that
they are then they respond

01:02:33.580 --> 01:02:35.590
and act responsibly as well.

01:02:35.590 --> 01:02:38.930
The big problem is that
the drinking age of 21

01:02:38.930 --> 01:02:41.680
forces our students to
have these underground,

01:02:41.680 --> 01:02:45.400
under supervised parties which
are happening off-campus.

01:02:45.400 --> 01:02:48.060
And that has just been
a real big challenge

01:02:48.060 --> 01:02:50.730
to address and regulate.

01:02:50.730 --> 01:02:53.920
That said, just on the
notion of Bulldog bash party,

01:02:53.920 --> 01:02:55.800
we're gonna do a miniature version of that

01:02:55.800 --> 01:02:59.413
for you all tonight on
Old Campus at 9:00 p.m.

01:03:00.340 --> 01:03:01.460
and to help you model--

01:03:01.460 --> 01:03:02.590
- Make sure to bring your ID.

01:03:02.590 --> 01:03:04.310
- Yeah, the party will be (mumbles),

01:03:04.310 --> 01:03:06.263
so please a pregame before you arrive.

01:03:07.440 --> 01:03:09.550
- We have three minutes,
so I'm gonna take,

01:03:09.550 --> 01:03:12.020
try to get to two questions,

01:03:12.020 --> 01:03:15.310
something that you really
wanna ask of this panel.

01:03:15.310 --> 01:03:17.230
Yep, right back there.

01:03:17.230 --> 01:03:18.063
Yes, you.

01:03:19.200 --> 01:03:20.123
- [Woman] Which one?

01:03:23.670 --> 01:03:27.353
- So my name is Sarah
Fox, I'm class of '73.

01:03:28.310 --> 01:03:31.800
We certainly were at Yale
during what you could call

01:03:31.800 --> 01:03:34.010
a hyper politicized time.

01:03:34.010 --> 01:03:37.050
But one of the things
that's been striking to me

01:03:37.050 --> 01:03:42.050
as we've walked around the
campus is how tranquil it is,

01:03:42.530 --> 01:03:44.010
how quiet it is?

01:03:44.010 --> 01:03:46.510
You don't see political posters,

01:03:46.510 --> 01:03:49.470
you don't see anything
about political events.

01:03:49.470 --> 01:03:52.570
There was as millions of
people around the world

01:03:52.570 --> 01:03:55.030
were demonstrating on
climate change yesterday,

01:03:55.030 --> 01:03:58.710
there was a little, if
there were 35 people

01:03:58.710 --> 01:04:02.300
out in front of Stirling
library during a little rally.

01:04:02.300 --> 01:04:04.000
(background noise drowns other sounds)

01:04:04.000 --> 01:04:04.940
They were out on the green

01:04:04.940 --> 01:04:06.850
and that's where Yale students were.

01:04:06.850 --> 01:04:08.940
So anyway, so that's my question I guess.

01:04:08.940 --> 01:04:11.200
Just what is the sense now

01:04:11.200 --> 01:04:15.350
about sort of political activism at Yale?

01:04:15.350 --> 01:04:18.810
Has that changed about women,
particularly at a time now

01:04:18.810 --> 01:04:21.010
where certain aspects of women's rights

01:04:21.010 --> 01:04:23.380
seem to be very much under threat,

01:04:23.380 --> 01:04:27.670
what is your sense of how the student body

01:04:27.670 --> 01:04:30.860
feels about itself as
engaged in the world?

01:04:30.860 --> 01:04:34.260
- I think we have a highly
engaged student body

01:04:34.260 --> 01:04:36.530
across the board in a lot of ways.

01:04:36.530 --> 01:04:37.550
The reason why you don't see posters

01:04:37.550 --> 01:04:40.140
is because everything's
on the internet right now.

01:04:40.140 --> 01:04:41.630
So they just, they do Facebook,

01:04:41.630 --> 01:04:45.500
they connect with each other
that way, it's less posters.

01:04:45.500 --> 01:04:47.630
But this is, you know
certainly if you've been here,

01:04:47.630 --> 01:04:48.580
first of all there's sleeping,

01:04:48.580 --> 01:04:51.010
that's why you don't
really see the right now.

01:04:51.010 --> 01:04:52.500
But for the last couple of years,

01:04:52.500 --> 01:04:56.310
certainly they have been
very much in the mix,

01:04:56.310 --> 01:04:57.740
many of them are spending a lot of time

01:04:57.740 --> 01:05:01.303
off-campus in campaigns,
they're doing work,

01:05:03.020 --> 01:05:06.480
you know in legal organizations.

01:05:06.480 --> 01:05:09.330
But we have a very politically savvy

01:05:09.330 --> 01:05:11.550
socially responsible group of students

01:05:11.550 --> 01:05:14.750
who represent a broad range of views.

01:05:14.750 --> 01:05:16.500
So don't let the tranquility

01:05:16.500 --> 01:05:18.780
which actually by the
way I enjoy right now

01:05:18.780 --> 01:05:21.010
as a Dean of Students,
don't let that fool you

01:05:21.010 --> 01:05:26.010
because they're very
much active, very active.

01:05:26.080 --> 01:05:28.050
And I think you would be proud

01:05:28.050 --> 01:05:29.690
that they are still
carrying on the tradition

01:05:29.690 --> 01:05:33.250
of being of service in a variety of ways,

01:05:33.250 --> 01:05:34.710
in a variety of countries
actually I would say.

01:05:34.710 --> 01:05:36.360
So they are definitely.

01:05:36.360 --> 01:05:37.193
Would you agree?

01:05:37.193 --> 01:05:38.026
Yeah.

01:05:38.026 --> 01:05:39.450
- As a former college president,

01:05:39.450 --> 01:05:42.050
the only thing I would
say is it was always great

01:05:42.050 --> 01:05:45.600
when our students were focusing
their political activities

01:05:45.600 --> 01:05:47.427
against the national
issues outside the college

01:05:47.427 --> 01:05:50.044
and not necessarily
against the administration.

01:05:50.044 --> 01:05:50.877
(audience laughing)

01:05:50.877 --> 01:05:51.710
- Exactly.

01:05:52.610 --> 01:05:54.754
- There's someone over
here who's been trying,

01:05:54.754 --> 01:05:56.743
yeah, right here.

01:05:58.890 --> 01:06:00.280
- This one.

01:06:00.280 --> 01:06:02.420
- Right in front, yes, no I--

01:06:02.420 --> 01:06:03.850
- Okay, thank you.

01:06:03.850 --> 01:06:06.420
I'm Ruth Gemmell, more '71.

01:06:06.420 --> 01:06:08.950
I'm just curious about
the question of leadership

01:06:08.950 --> 01:06:12.700
and what happens to people
after they leave here

01:06:12.700 --> 01:06:14.270
and how you define that these days

01:06:14.270 --> 01:06:18.570
particularly with the
emphasis on pre-professional

01:06:20.110 --> 01:06:23.940
partly because of the cost
of living, cost of college,

01:06:23.940 --> 01:06:26.720
a lot of more students
are on financial aid.

01:06:26.720 --> 01:06:29.470
So like everybody does what they do here

01:06:29.470 --> 01:06:33.060
and then they go work for
whoever can pay them the most.

01:06:33.060 --> 01:06:36.163
So I just, since we talk about leadership,

01:06:37.430 --> 01:06:39.470
how do you define it now?

01:06:39.470 --> 01:06:41.900
- So we track what students are doing

01:06:41.900 --> 01:06:44.490
after graduation very carefully,

01:06:44.490 --> 01:06:47.980
we have good statistics
starting from the class of 2013

01:06:47.980 --> 01:06:52.270
and so for that class we even
have data from five years out.

01:06:52.270 --> 01:06:54.540
You know, basically, I
think we can be very proud

01:06:54.540 --> 01:06:58.400
of our young alumni that they, you know,

01:06:58.400 --> 01:07:03.140
that they engage very broadly
including in many areas

01:07:03.140 --> 01:07:06.480
of public service at nonprofits.

01:07:06.480 --> 01:07:09.810
To further encourage
that, president Salovey

01:07:09.810 --> 01:07:13.520
approved this new program
called Domestic Summer Awards

01:07:13.520 --> 01:07:16.180
which will pay students on financial aid

01:07:16.180 --> 01:07:20.070
to work for a nonprofit
organization over the summers

01:07:20.070 --> 01:07:24.680
so that these financial
concerns would not constrain

01:07:24.680 --> 01:07:26.910
the types of education and experiences

01:07:26.910 --> 01:07:29.390
that they would pursue
while they're here at Yale.

01:07:29.390 --> 01:07:30.670
And we're seeing the impact, you know,

01:07:30.670 --> 01:07:33.080
I think we're gonna see the
impact of that continuing

01:07:33.080 --> 01:07:35.920
even after graduation.

01:07:35.920 --> 01:07:37.900
And also just one other
additional statistic

01:07:37.900 --> 01:07:40.778
about our students is
that a lot of them go work

01:07:40.778 --> 01:07:45.140
but by five years out from
graduation over half of them

01:07:45.140 --> 01:07:47.870
come do some form of graduate

01:07:47.870 --> 01:07:50.190
or professional school education.

01:07:50.190 --> 01:07:52.080
So I think that's a pretty
cool statistics as well.

01:07:52.080 --> 01:07:54.910
- And Yale's financial aid
policies are incredibly generous

01:07:54.910 --> 01:07:58.330
and so students are able to
graduate without loan burdens.

01:07:58.330 --> 01:08:00.470
So those do not constrain decisions

01:08:00.470 --> 01:08:02.372
about what they can do afterwards.

01:08:02.372 --> 01:08:04.386
(audience applauding)

01:08:04.386 --> 01:08:06.930
- So very quickly, do
you think the definition

01:08:06.930 --> 01:08:10.150
of leadership has changed with women

01:08:10.150 --> 01:08:14.300
being so much a part of Yale

01:08:14.300 --> 01:08:17.560
or is it the measures are still the same

01:08:17.560 --> 01:08:21.630
and women or meeting or
exceeding those measures.

01:08:21.630 --> 01:08:23.086
- You know I ask this question,

01:08:23.086 --> 01:08:24.960
I'm like 'cause I love
students working in my office

01:08:24.960 --> 01:08:28.383
about what constitute
leadership at Yale College.

01:08:29.460 --> 01:08:32.200
And what they said is, you
know, we typically think

01:08:32.200 --> 01:08:34.300
of leadership positions on
campus as being the head

01:08:34.300 --> 01:08:38.010
of the YDN, YCC, those organizations.

01:08:38.010 --> 01:08:40.100
And most recently, the most recent woman

01:08:40.100 --> 01:08:42.530
who was head of the YCC
was actually just last year

01:08:42.530 --> 01:08:45.270
and I think they've been a 10 year hiatus

01:08:45.270 --> 01:08:47.010
since the last woman who was a head of it.

01:08:47.010 --> 01:08:48.760
But most of the students would say,

01:08:48.760 --> 01:08:51.100
that yes those organizations are wonderful

01:08:51.100 --> 01:08:54.540
but what they see as
leadership now are leadership

01:08:54.540 --> 01:08:57.030
in the 600 organizations that we now have,

01:08:57.030 --> 01:08:59.980
leadership on issues of social justice

01:08:59.980 --> 01:09:04.040
and a lot of the things that
are really moving the campus

01:09:04.040 --> 01:09:06.170
in ways that sometimes
are uncomfortable for us

01:09:06.170 --> 01:09:09.370
are really being led by women,
and honestly women of color

01:09:09.370 --> 01:09:10.520
are really stepping into the place,

01:09:10.520 --> 01:09:11.980
talk about intersectionality.

01:09:11.980 --> 01:09:14.330
So I think for this generation,

01:09:14.330 --> 01:09:17.020
the concept of leader is broader
than we would typically say

01:09:17.020 --> 01:09:20.210
and that most students see
themselves as being leaders

01:09:20.210 --> 01:09:21.800
in some way shape or form

01:09:21.800 --> 01:09:24.930
mostly because they all wanna
make some kind of an impact.

01:09:24.930 --> 01:09:27.210
And what I think the shift has been

01:09:27.210 --> 01:09:29.270
is actually they wanna
make an impact at home.

01:09:29.270 --> 01:09:31.550
So many more of them are going back home

01:09:31.550 --> 01:09:33.070
to make an impact that's
probably this shift

01:09:33.070 --> 01:09:34.810
and that's where they see their leadership

01:09:35.810 --> 01:09:36.850
as having (mumbles).

01:09:36.850 --> 01:09:38.980
- You know this leadership has a long

01:09:38.980 --> 01:09:40.720
and strange history if you think about it,

01:09:40.720 --> 01:09:43.090
but everyone smirks if
you say 1,000 were leaders

01:09:43.090 --> 01:09:44.720
but everyone knows a school like Yale

01:09:44.720 --> 01:09:45.987
doesn't just accept some students

01:09:45.987 --> 01:09:50.230
and say tries to develop
the talent of students

01:09:50.230 --> 01:09:52.590
who are gonna have
disproportionate impact.

01:09:52.590 --> 01:09:55.000
But also these students
have lived with pressure.

01:09:55.000 --> 01:09:56.380
To apply to a college nowadays,

01:09:56.380 --> 01:09:57.500
you have to have been the president

01:09:57.500 --> 01:09:59.330
of at least three clubs in high school.

01:09:59.330 --> 01:10:01.970
Let's just say, you've been
pushed into a certain definition

01:10:01.970 --> 01:10:05.610
of what leadership is which
means I have the foremost role,

01:10:05.610 --> 01:10:08.830
I have a certain prestige
or something of that sort.

01:10:08.830 --> 01:10:11.290
I think by the time people
get out and in life,

01:10:11.290 --> 01:10:14.220
they understand that's not
really what leadership was about,

01:10:14.220 --> 01:10:16.100
it wasn't about the position you held,

01:10:16.100 --> 01:10:18.600
it's about thinking about
how people take their gifts

01:10:18.600 --> 01:10:20.690
and go have a disproportional impact,

01:10:20.690 --> 01:10:22.210
how they make other people smarter,

01:10:22.210 --> 01:10:24.160
how they ask more interesting questions,

01:10:24.160 --> 01:10:26.940
how they make people think
that impossible ideas

01:10:26.940 --> 01:10:29.710
might be less impossible than they appear.

01:10:29.710 --> 01:10:30.990
And you know if I look in this room

01:10:30.990 --> 01:10:33.670
and see people who I can see
with careers in medicine,

01:10:33.670 --> 01:10:36.430
with careers in architecture,
with careers in academics,

01:10:36.430 --> 01:10:38.400
the world needs all of those people,

01:10:38.400 --> 01:10:41.000
it doesn't need everyone to be
the president of those things

01:10:41.000 --> 01:10:43.240
but it needs people to be
the leader in more or less

01:10:43.240 --> 01:10:44.516
the sense I have described.

01:10:44.516 --> 01:10:45.349
- I think students would agree,

01:10:45.349 --> 01:10:46.485
I think they say leadership is service.

01:10:46.485 --> 01:10:48.920
(all applauding).

01:10:48.920 --> 01:10:50.710
- That's a great note to end on.

01:10:50.710 --> 01:10:52.920
Thank you everyone for being here

01:10:52.920 --> 01:10:55.000
and thank you panelist.

01:10:55.000 --> 01:10:57.597
(audience applauding)

01:10:57.597 --> 01:11:01.067
(background noise drowns other sound)

